// The requirements of the language as a DAG (SREQ-00012-1): the purpose at
// the root, then the domains of the language, then the entities, then one
// requirement per characteristic; the rules that hold for every instance of
// a category are constraints, hung where they apply. Nesting is refinement.
// Every leaf names the oracle test that fixes it, and the member of ds that
// implements it realises it. The grammar box holds the rules of the text
// (grammar/grammar.ds). This file knows nothing of who consumes the language:
// the connections to the system's SREQ live in design/wiring.ds, the
// consumer's file (decided 2026-10-26).

package driftspec@ds {

  requirement REQ-00012-1 {
    tags: language
    status: approved
    verification VER-00048-1 {
      status: approved
      title: Unit tests confirm every oracle corpus of the language means what it marks
      method: test
      tests:
        tests/integration/ds/check/test_static.cpp::TestStatic.TheOracleFindingsAndNothingElse
        tests/integration/ds/check/test_behaviour.cpp::TestBehaviour.TheOracleFindings
        tests/integration/ds/check/test_artefacts.cpp::TestArtefacts.TheOracleFindingsAndNothingElse
    }
    title: The language shall specify a software design as text: its high-level design, its low-level design, the artefacts about it and the views of it
    rationale:
      One text, one meaning, for the whole of a design: what the tool loads,
      checks, draws, compares and asks about.

    // ======================= metamodel ====================================
    requirement REQ-00066-1 {
      tags: language, metamodel
      status: approved
      title: The language shall be declared once as a metamodel: every kind with its fields, flags and parents, every relation with the kinds it joins
      text:
        Each kind names the fields it carries, each with a type (text, name,
        names, type, params, number, status, patterns) and who sets it (the
        author, the syntax, or the checker), the flags it takes and the kinds
        it may be declared inside; a field whose name ends in * is a family
        (local.*, exit *, contract.*). Each relation names the kinds it may go
        from and to. Everything the binder and the checker put in a model is
        something the metamodel declares.
      rationale:
        Owner, 2026-11-05: DriftSpec has its own data model, not argos's. The
        binder wrote some fifty field names that nothing declared, and every
        reader parsed them again; the render of the whole model (DS-67) needs
        to know what exists before drawing it.
      verification VER-00072-3 {
        status: approved
        title: Integration tests confirm that the design of ds and every example corpus are made only of what the metamodel declares, a relation the language does not allow is reported and not bound, and the metamodel places each kind where the grammar does and declares every flag the binder sets
        method: test
        tests:
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_conformance.cpp::TestMetamodelConformance.EveryEntityAndRelationIsOneTheMetamodelDeclares
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_conformance.cpp::TestMetamodelConformance.ARelationTheLanguageDoesNotAllowIsReportedAndNotBound
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_audit_defects.cpp::TestAuditDefects.AnAttributeThatOwnsAnInterfaceIsToldToWriteRefs
          tests/integration/ds/model/test_metamodel_truth.cpp::TestMetamodelTruth.EveryKindIsDeclaredInWhatTheGrammarLetsItBeWrittenIn
          tests/integration/ds/model/test_metamodel_truth.cpp::TestMetamodelTruth.EveryFlagTheBinderSetsIsOneItsKindDeclares
      }
      verification VER-00075-1 {
        status: approved
        title: Integration tests confirm parameters and arguments are read once into structures that agree with their text, over the design of ds and every example
        method: test
        tests:
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_conformance.cpp::TestMetamodelConformance.ParametersAndArgumentsAreReadOnceAndAgreeWithTheirText
      }
      requirement REQ-00067-1 {
        tags: language, metamodel, fields
        status: approved
        title: A field that its kind does not declare shall be reported as W-FIELD-UNKNOWN
        text:
          A key: value an entity carries that the metamodel does not declare
          for its kind (a typo, or a field the language does not know) is a
          warning at the entity, naming the field and the kind. It is kept in
          the model: the warning does not drop it.
        rationale:
          DS-22: a misspelt field entered silently. The metamodel now says
          which fields exist, so the checker can say which do not.
        verification VER-00073-1 {
          status: approved
          title: Integration tests confirm an undeclared field is reported and a declared one is not, and the diag corpus marks its layout field
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_conformance.cpp::TestMetamodelConformance.AFieldItsKindDoesNotDeclareIsReportedAndADeclaredOneIsNot
        }
      }

      requirement REQ-00115-2 {
        tags: language, metamodel, fields
        status: approved
        title: A closed field carrying a value it does not take shall be reported as W-FIELD-VALUE
        text:
          The metamodel names the values of its closed fields: status (draft,
          approved, obsolete), a verification's method and level, a diagram's
          kind. Any other value is a warning at the entity, naming the
          field, the value and the values it takes; an empty value is left alone.
          The value is kept in the model.
          Revision 2 (DS-231): a diagram has no level any more; the cut is written
          with close: (REQ-00126-1).
        rationale:
          Model audit 2026-11, findings G4 and G3 (DS-211): status: aproved
          passed, stopped counting as approved and so switched
          W-REQ-NO-VERIFICATION off; kind: clas fell back to a class diagram and
          level: package was ignored in silence.
        verification VER-00131-2 {
          status: approved
          title: Integration tests confirm a closed field with a value it does not take is reported and one it takes is not, and the diag corpus marks a component kind
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_conformance.cpp::TestMetamodelConformance.AClosedFieldWithAValueItDoesNotTakeIsReportedAndOneItTakesIsNot
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_conformance.cpp::TestMetamodelConformance.EachNameOfAClosedListIsTakenOrSaid
        }
      }
    }

    // ======================= workspace ====================================
    requirement REQ-00053-3 {
      tags: language, workspace
      status: approved
      title: A specification shall be a directory of .ds files read as one, in any order
      text:
        Files are read whole and are not units of meaning; a link nobody
        declares (an annotation, the subject of a behaviour) is an expected node
        reported once (W-REF-UNDECLARED) and never stops the load; a name in a
        type, a base, a bound or a call is text instead (REQ-00024-3); only
        syntax (E-SYNTAX) or an encoding that is not UTF-8 (E-ENCODING) stops
        a file.
      rationale:
        A specification that lives per file cannot say that two files talk
        about the same thing (R1, R6). Revision 2 (DS-18, 2026-11-20): names in
        types are text. Revision 3 (DS-411, 2026-11-24): a file that is not
        UTF-8 stops too, as a syntax error does.
      verification VER-00041-2 {
        title: Integration tests confirm a specification is a directory of .ds files read as one, in any order
        status: approved
        method: test
        tests:
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_checker.cpp::TestChecker.UndeclaredReferencesBecomeExpectedNodesReportedOnce
          tests/integration/ds/parse/test_workspace_parsing.cpp::TestWorkspaceParsing.TheFilesOfADirectoryAreReadAsOneInAnyOrder
          tests/integration/ds/parse/test_workspace_parsing.cpp::TestWorkspaceParsing.AFileThatParsesReportsNoSyntaxErrorBesideOneThatDoesNot
      }
      constraint CST-00002-1 {
        tags: language, workspace
        status: approved
        category: language
        bok: BOK-00004-1
        title: Every entity shall have one identity, and a second declaration of it shall be E-DUPLICATE-IDENTITY
        rationale: R5: identity across the workspace is what makes references, diagrams and diffs mean the same entity everywhere.
      }
      requirement REQ-00164-1 {
        tags: language, workspace, behaviour
        status: approved
        title: A member named after a behaviour its holder has shall be E-NAME-RESERVED, and the behaviour keep its identity
        text:
          A state machine is its subject's identity with ::state, and a sequence its subject's with
          ::sequence; a member written with that name takes the identity of the behaviour when the
          behaviour is written too. The member is reported where it is written, once, and the
          behaviour keeps the identity whichever of the two is written first, with nothing else said
          of the collision. A member named state or sequence whose holder has no such behaviour is
          an ordinary member.
        rationale:
          Owner, 2026-11-18 (DS-222, model audit finding I1): the names are the language's, not a new
          separator, so the identities users write in diagrams (Cart::add::sequence/sold-out) do not
          change. Reported only where the two meet: DriftSpec's own design has nine members called
          state or sequence (Identity::state(), the live session's GET /state) with no behaviour of
          that name, and they stay as the code has them.
        verification VER-00180-1 {
          status: approved
          title: Integration tests confirm a method named state beside its class's state machine is E-NAME-RESERVED once, the machine keeps its identity, and without a machine the method is ordinary
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_audit_defects.cpp::TestAuditDefects.AMemberNamedAfterABehaviourOfItsHolderIsToldSoAndTheBehaviourKeepsItsIdentity
        }
      }
      constraint CST-00003-2 {
        tags: language, workspace
        status: approved
        category: language
        bok: BOK-00004-1
        title: Every entity shall be defined once and extended only by blocks of annotations
        text:
          An extension adds relations and never touches the definition; an extension of nothing is an expected node,
          except a class: written with annotations only and defined by no other block, it is the class, empty, and its
          scope contains it (revision 2, DS-20: an exception class that only carries its requirements).
        rationale: A box writes its requirements without knowing who consumes them (2026-10-26).
      }
    }

    // ======================= HLD ==========================================
    requirement REQ-00013-1 {
      tags: language, hld
      status: approved
      verification VER-00049-1 {
        status: approved
        title: Unit tests confirm the HLD is modelled as nested packages
        method: test
        tests:
          tests/integration/ds/bind/test_packages.cpp::TestPackages.EveryPackageLevelAddsItsNameWithAt
      }
      title: The language shall model the high-level design as packages, the organisation above the implementation
      rationale:
        The HLD is where a system is divided into deployable units and
        boundaries; argos reads it as boxes that own namespaces.

      requirement REQ-00014-1 {
        tags: language, hld
        status: approved
        verification VER-00050-1 {
          status: approved
          title: Unit tests confirm a package is a box holding packages, modules and artefacts
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/bind/test_packages.cpp::TestPackages.TheFlatFormIsTheSameIdentityAsNesting
            tests/integration/ds/bind/test_packages.cpp::TestPackages.LevelByPlacementThroughTheLevels
        }
        title: A package shall be a box of the high-level design: a black box that holds packages, modules and artefacts
        rationale: R1, R2; nested packages 2026-10-26.

        requirement REQ-00015-1 {
          tags: language, hld
          status: approved
          title: A package shall nest in a package, written nested or reopened flat by its full path
          text:
            package app { package core { } } and package app@core { } are the
            same box; the flat form needs the outer package declared somewhere.
          verification VER-00012-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a package nests in a package, written nested or reopened flat by its full path
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/bind/test_packages.cpp::TestPackages.EveryPackageLevelAddsItsNameWithAt
              tests/integration/ds/bind/test_packages.cpp::TestPackages.TheFlatFormIsTheSameIdentityAsNesting
              tests/integration/ds/bind/test_packages.cpp::TestPackages.AnOuterPackageNobodyDeclaresIsExpected
          }
        }
        constraint CST-00004-1 {
          tags: language, hld
          status: approved
          category: language
          bok: BOK-00004-1
          title: Every identity shall name its packages with @ and its namespaces with ::, the box that holds the modules rooting the code's namespace
          text:
            app@core@model::Entity: boxes with @, namespaces with ::; outer
            boxes are organisation and drop out of the qualified name.
          rationale: decided 2026-10-26 with argos's reading by namespace (R4).
        }
        requirement REQ-00017-2 {
          tags: language, hld
          status: approved
          title: A package or a module shall realise requirements and carry constraints through annotations, like a class
          text: @requirement and @constraint in the package or module body.
          rationale:
            Revises REQ-00017-1, which named the package only: the metamodel let
            a module answer to a requirement but the grammar did not let one
            write it (DS-82, found by a newcomer agent, 2026-11-06).
          verification VER-00014-2 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a package or a module realises requirements and carries constraints through annotations, like a class
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/bind/test_packages.cpp::TestPackages.ABoxRealisesRequirementsLikeAClass
              tests/integration/ds/bind/test_module_annotations.cpp::TestModuleAnnotations.AModuleAnswersToTheRequirementItsBodyNames
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00084-1 {
          tags: language, hld
          status: approved
          title: A package or a module shall say what its code is written in, and what it holds shall be written in the same unless it says otherwise
          text:
            language: cpp in a package or a module; cpp, c, python, typescript or lua, the
            languages argos reads. What a box holds is written in its language unless it says
            another, and the check fills the language down, so every package and module of the
            model carries it. One ds does not know, or two at once (a reopened box that says
            two), is W-LANGUAGE-UNKNOWN. A box that says nothing and is held by nothing that
            says has no language.
          rationale:
            Owner, 2026-11-06 (DS-86): nothing said which technology each package used, and
            DriftSpec designs both C++ and Python (iris; the Python face of ds itself). Knowing
            it, argos picks the reader of each box and writes its types the way that language
            does.
          verification VER-00093-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a box's language is held by what it holds unless it says otherwise, and an unknown one or two at once is a finding
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_language.cpp::TestLanguage.WhatABoxHoldsIsWrittenInItsLanguageUnlessItSaysOtherwise
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_language.cpp::TestLanguage.ABoxThatSaysNothingAndHoldsNothingThatSaysHasNoLanguage
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_language.cpp::TestLanguage.ALanguageDsDoesNotKnowOrTwoAtOnceIsAFinding
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00018-1 {
          tags: language, hld
          status: approved
          title: The outermost package shall be the system, and the level of an artefact shall follow from where it is declared
          text:
            In the outermost package an artefact is of the system; in an inner
            package or a module, of that level. No keyword, no field.
          verification VER-00015-2 {
            title: Integration tests confirm the outermost package is the system, and the level of an artefact follows from where it is declared
            status: approved
            text: Revision 2 (DS-184) cites the integration test alone; the unit test of a scope is the class's own.
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/bind/test_packages.cpp::TestPackages.LevelByPlacementThroughTheLevels
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00019-1 {
          tags: language, hld
          status: approved
          title: A package shall draw as a container of its modules in a class diagram
          verification VER-00016-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a package draws as a container of its modules in a class diagram
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/bind/test_packages.cpp::TestPackages.NestedPackagesAreContainersOfTheDrawing
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00020-2 {
          tags: language, hld
          status: approved
          title: A package shall declare the external interfaces of what it builds, with their kind and typed operations
          text:
            interface shell { kind: cli; operation buy(sku: string) -> int { exit 2: refused } }, at any
            level of packages. The kind and the fields of an operation are data, not grammar, so a new
            kind changes no syntax. Quality is a requirement or a constraint linked to the interface or
            to an operation. Replaces boundary (DS-54): an interface of the high level design is the
            external interface of a process, or of a library when it is binary.
          rationale: Owner, 2026-11-05. The syntax is self-contained, with no other formats or parsers.
          verification VER-00064-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a package declares the external interfaces of what it builds, with their kind and typed operations
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_interfaces.cpp::TestInterfaces.AnInterfaceHoldsItsTypedOperationsAndTheirFields
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_interfaces.cpp::TestInterfaces.RequirementsAndConstraintsLinkToAnInterfaceOrAnOperation
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_interfaces.cpp::TestInterfaces.AnInterfaceIsDrawnAsABoxWithItsOperations
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00088-1 {
          tags: language, hld, dependencies
          status: approved
          title: A package, a module or a class shall declare that it uses an interface another box offers, in its own body or from another file
          text:
            @consumes sistema@broker@publish in a box binds a consumes relation from the box to the interface; the
            box that offers the interface is the provider. Like every annotation it binds the same
            written in the box or in an extension block anywhere else, so the wiring of a system can
            live in a file of its own (as design/wiring.ds does for requirements) and a design brought
            in whole can be connected from above without being touched. What an annotation means is
            read from the metamodel's table of annotations, declared once.
          rationale:
            Owner, 2026-11-07 (DS-96): designing a distributed system live, the high level design could
            not say that the service uses the broker's publish interface; the boxes stood apart. The
            Owner asked for the link to be external, like the wiring of requirements.
          verification VER-00097-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a box uses an interface of another, written in its body or in another file, and a use of something that is no interface is reported
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/bind/test_uses.cpp::TestUses.APackageAModuleOrAClassUsesAnInterfaceOfAnotherBox
              tests/integration/ds/bind/test_uses.cpp::TestUses.AUseWrittenInAnotherFileBindsTheSame
              tests/integration/ds/bind/test_uses.cpp::TestUses.AUseOfSomethingThatIsNoInterfaceIsReportedAndNotBound
              tests/integration/ds/bind/test_uses.cpp::TestUses.AnAnnotationMeansWhatTheMetamodelSaysWhereverItIsWritten
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00093-1 {
          tags: language, hld, dependencies
          status: approved
          title: A module shall offer interfaces like a package, and a behaviour shall call an interface's operation through a local, a parameter or an attribute typed by it
          text:
            interface port { ... } inside module face is p@face::port, offered by the module. A
            behaviour reaches an interface's operation as it reaches a method: local ds: p@engine
            then -> ds.check(text), or a parameter or an attribute typed by the interface; the call
            names the operation, so the consumer depends on the contract and not on the class that
            realises it. In the viewer a module's interfaces come first inside it.
          rationale:
            Owner, 2026-11-07 (DS-92): the Python face called the engine's class Api because a
            sequence could not call engine; and a dependency between two modules of one package had
            no interface to go through.
          verification VER-00102-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a module offers interfaces and a behaviour calls an interface's operation through a local, a parameter or an attribute
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_interface_calls.cpp::TestInterfaceCalls.AModuleDeclaresAnInterfaceAndOffersIt
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_interface_calls.cpp::TestInterfaceCalls.ABehaviourCallsAnInterfaceOperationThroughALocal
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_interface_calls.cpp::TestInterfaceCalls.ThroughAParameterOrAnAttributeTypedByTheInterface
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_interface_calls.cpp::TestInterfaceCalls.TheSceneDrawsAModulesInterfaceInsideIt
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00094-2 {
          tags: language, hld, dependencies, check
          status: approved
          title: The checker shall compare what a box declares it uses with what its behaviours call, and report what is not declared, what is declared and never called, and a call that goes around an interface
          text:
            A call to an operation of an interface another box offers needs a @consumes of that interface
            on the caller or on a box it lives in, written there or in a wiring file
            (W-CONSUMES-UNDECLARED). A @consumes that none of the box's behaviours calls is dead, said only once
            the box has behaviours (W-CONSUMES-UNUSED). A call to a method of a class that realises an
            interface, from outside the class's module, goes around the contract when the method
            answers an operation of that interface (W-CONSUMES-BYPASS); a method the interface does not
            offer cannot be called through it and is no bypass (DS-106). An
            interface's own sequences realise it, and a box calling an interface it offers depends on
            nothing.
          rationale: Owner, 2026-11-07 (DS-96, DS-92): declared dependencies are only worth what the behaviour confirms.
          verification VER-00103-2 {
            title: Integration tests confirm undeclared, unused and bypassing dependencies are reported, and what is declared, planned or realised is not
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_dependency_checks.cpp::TestDependencyChecks.ACallToAnotherBoxsInterfaceNeedsAUseOnTheCallerOrWhereItLives
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_dependency_checks.cpp::TestDependencyChecks.AUseOnTheModuleOrInAWiringBlockOnItsPackageCoversTheCall
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_dependency_checks.cpp::TestDependencyChecks.AUseNoBehaviourCallsIsDeadOnceTheBoxHasBehaviours
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_dependency_checks.cpp::TestDependencyChecks.CallingTheClassBehindAnInterfaceFromOutsideGoesAroundIt
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_dependency_checks.cpp::TestDependencyChecks.AnInterfacesOwnSequenceRealisesItAndNothingIsReported
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_dependency_checks.cpp::TestDependencyChecks.AMethodTheInterfaceDoesNotOfferIsNoBypass
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00119-2 {
          tags: language, hld, interfaces, check
          status: approved
          title: A method that answers an operation of an interface its class realises shall take and give what the operation does, or be reported as W-INTERFACE-SIGNATURE
          text:
            When a class realises an interface (@interface) and declares, or inherits, a method named
            like one of the interface's operations, their parameters and returns are compared as the
            idea each type commits to: qualifiers and spaces dropped, the rest as the code writes it.
            A difference is a warning at the method. An operation the class does not declare is no
            finding: that is absence.
          rationale:
            Model audit 2026-11, finding G5 (DS-215): a class realising keeper declared feed(animal:
            int, extra: bool) against feed(animal: string) and nothing was said, while between an
            operation and its use case the signatures were compared. It is the link from the
            high-level design to the low-level one. Revision 2 (DS-18, 2026-11-20): no neutral name
            is read any more; the types are the code's text.
          verification VER-00135-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a method that answers an interface operation with another signature is reported, one that says the same idea is not, and an operation the class does not declare is no finding
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_dependency_checks.cpp::TestRealisation.AMethodThatAnswersAnInterfaceOperationWithAnotherSignatureIsReported
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00081-1 {
          tags: language, hld, usecase
          status: approved
          title: A use case shall be declared as a contract with its outcomes, realised by interface operations and specialised by a specific use case
          text:
            usecase UC-00001-1 greet(name: string) -> Greeting { outcome default: ...; outcome
            empty-name -> none: ... } is an artefact identified by its id with its revision: a
            title, a status, the requirements that bind it (@requirement), an optional signature
            and its outcomes, default the ending that goes well and every other one a scenario.
            It names no interface: an interface operation realises it (operation greet(...) :
            UC-00001-1), whoever drives that interface. A specific use case specialises a
            generic one after a colon and keeps its signature, its outcomes and its
            requirements. A design without use cases owes none.
          rationale:
            Owner, 2026-11-06 (DS-77): a use case is its own entity, close to a requirement; it
            may be bound by several requirements and triggered from several interfaces of the
            high level design, and it should be seen whole from each of them. No actor: the
            interface already says what drives it.
          verification VER-00090-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm hola realises its use cases from both interfaces, a specific one inherits the outcomes of its generic, and a design without use cases owes none
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_usecases.cpp::TestUseCases.HolaRealisesItsUseCasesFromBothInterfacesAndChecksClean
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_usecases.cpp::TestUseCases.ASpecificUseCaseInheritsTheOutcomesOfItsGeneric
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_usecases.cpp::TestUseCases.ADesignWithoutUseCasesOwesNone
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00082-1 {
          tags: language, hld, usecase, check
          status: approved
          title: The check shall hold every operation that realises a use case to its contract, outcome by outcome
          text:
            An operation that takes or gives something else than the use case it realises, or a
            specific use case that does so against its generic, is W-USECASE-SIGNATURE. An
            outcome, its own or inherited, that the operation handles with no scenario is
            W-USECASE-OUTCOME-UNHANDLED; a scenario the operation handles that no outcome names
            is W-SCENARIO-NO-OUTCOME. A use case no operation realises, directly or through one
            that specialises it, is W-USECASE-UNREALISED; a realisation or a specialisation of an
            id that is no use case is W-USECASE-UNDECLARED.
          rationale:
            The use case is what makes "from which interfaces, and how does it end" checkable:
            on ds's own design these checks caught the outcomes the command line changed.
          verification VER-00091-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm each way an operation can break its use case is a finding, and keeping the contract is none
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_usecases.cpp::TestUseCases.AnOperationThatHandlesEveryOutcomeKeepsTheContract
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_usecases.cpp::TestUseCases.AnOperationThatGivesSomethingElseBreaksTheContract
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_usecases.cpp::TestUseCases.AnOutcomeNoScenarioHandlesIsReported
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_usecases.cpp::TestUseCases.AScenarioNoOutcomeNamesIsReported
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_usecases.cpp::TestUseCases.AUseCaseNothingRealisesAndAnUnknownOneAreReported
          }
        }
      }
    }

    // ======================= LLD ==========================================
    requirement REQ-00021-1 {
      tags: language, lld
      status: approved
      verification VER-00051-1 {
        status: approved
        title: Unit tests confirm the LLD is modelled as modules of classes and behaviours: the static oracle
        method: test
        tests:
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_static.cpp::TestStatic.TheOracleFindingsAndNothingElse
      }
      title: The language shall model the low-level design as modules of classes and their behaviours
      rationale: The white box: what argos compares with the code.

      // ---- module ----
      requirement REQ-00022-1 {
        tags: language, lld, module
        status: approved
        verification VER-00052-1 {
          status: approved
          title: Unit tests confirm a module is a namespace that reopens
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/bind/test_binder.cpp::TestBinder.ModulesCanBeReopenedAcrossFilesButClassesCannot
        }
        title: A module shall be a namespace that reopens across files and resolves names outward
        rationale: R2, R9, R10.

        requirement REQ-00023-1 {
          tags: language, lld, module
          status: approved
          title: A bare name shall resolve from the innermost scope outward, a qualified name across modules, a pkg@ one across packages
          text: No includes, no using; what already encloses you is omitted.
          verification VER-00017-2 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a bare name resolves from the innermost scope outward, a qualified name across modules, a pkg@ one across packages
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_checker.cpp::TestChecker.ResolvesInnermostScopeFirst
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_audit_defects.cpp::TestAuditDefects.ABareNameFindsTheClassBesideItBeforeARootPackageOfItsName
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_static.cpp::TestStatic.CrossModuleReferencesResolveByTheModuleOrByThePackage
              tests/integration/ds/bind/test_binder.cpp::TestBinder.ModulesCanBeReopenedAcrossFilesButClassesCannot
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00024-3 {
          tags: language, lld, module
          status: approved
          title: A name the design does not declare shall be text in a type, a base, a bound or a call, relating nothing and reported nowhere
          text:
            A design declares what it designs and nothing else. A name read out of a
            type, a base, a template bound or a call that resolves to nothing the
            design declares is text: no relation, no expected node, no finding
            (std::string, Exception, subprocess::run). There is no extern module:
            a design that still writes one fails with E-SYNTAX until it is migrated.
          rationale:
            R11; the extern catalogue of 2026-10-26. Revision 3 (DS-18, Owner
            2026-11-20): allow incompleteness, say incoherence. Designs inherit
            from and call the standard and outside libraries all the time, and
            declaring them cost preludes that existed only to be named; a
            misspelt name of the design is caught by argos against the code and
            shows as a missing arrow in the drawing.
          verification VER-00018-3 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a name the design does not declare is text in a type, a base, a bound or a call, relating nothing and reported nowhere
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_static.cpp::TestStatic.ANameNobodyDesignsIsTextAndRelatesNothing
              tests/integration/ds/bind/test_packages.cpp::TestPackages.ANameNobodyDesignsIsTextAndSaysNothing
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_types_as_text.cpp::TestTypesAsText.OnlyTheNamesTheDesignDeclaresRelateTheRestIsText
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00169-2 {
          tags: language, lld, module
          status: approved
          title: A bare name that no scope declares shall resolve to the one class of that name in its package, and warn when two share it
          text:
            After the scopes, from the innermost outward, a bare name (no :: and no
            @) is looked for among the classes and enums of the package it is
            written in, at any depth of its modules: the one of that name and arity
            is it, and another package is never asked. When two or more share it,
            it relates nothing and W-NAME-AMBIGUOUS is reported once where it is
            written, naming every candidate by its identity. A name written from
            the top package, a name nobody declares and a template parameter say
            nothing. It is the rule argos applies to the code, so Plan, written as
            a Python module imports it from a sibling module, finds the class there.
          rationale:
            DS-18, Owner 2026-11-20, agreed with the argos side: a type written as
            the code writes it names imported classes bare, and without this rule
            the design of a Python package lost every relation between its modules.
            Revision 2 (DS-325, Owner 2026-11-20): two candidates are an
            ambiguity, an incoherence, and an incoherence is said.
          verification VER-00186-2 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a bare name no scope declares is the one class of that name in its package, warns once naming the candidates when two share it, and is never one of another package
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_types_as_text.cpp::TestTypesAsText.ABareNameNoScopeDeclaresIsTheOneClassOfThePackage
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_types_as_text.cpp::TestTypesAsText.ABareNameTwoClassesOfThePackageShareWarnsOnceForItsUse
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_static.cpp::TestStatic.CrossModuleReferencesResolveByTheModuleOrByThePackage
          }
        }
      }

      // ---- class ----
      requirement REQ-00025-1 {
        tags: language, lld, class
        status: approved
        verification VER-00053-1 {
          status: approved
          title: Unit tests confirm classes, dataclasses and enums have identities and members
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_static.cpp::TestStatic.EnumsAndTemplatesHaveIdentities
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_static.cpp::TestStatic.DefaultsAndModifiersAreKept
        }
        title: A class shall be a type of the design: class, dataclass or enum, with members
        rationale: R12, R14, R19.

        requirement REQ-00026-2 {
          tags: language, lld, class
          status: approved
          title: A member shall carry visibility, modifiers, a name, a type and an optional default
          text:
            + # -, then abstract | static | const for methods, or owns | refs |
            shares and many | optional for attributes, then the name; a method
            named like its class is a constructor; name: type = default, the
            type as the code writes it. *args and **kwargs are parameter names
            like any other. Members are identified by name: an overload is one
            member.
          rationale: Revision 2 (DS-18, 2026-11-20): many and optional say the multiplicity, and a type is the code's text.
          verification VER-00019-2 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a member carries visibility, modifiers, a name, a type and an optional default
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_static.cpp::TestStatic.DefaultsAndModifiersAreKept
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_types_as_text.cpp::TestTypesAsText.MultiplicityIsWrittenInFrontOfTheName
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_types_as_text.cpp::TestTypesAsText.OnlyTheNamesTheDesignDeclaresRelateTheRestIsText
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00170-1 {
          tags: language, lld, class
          status: approved
          title: An attribute written with two ownerships or two multiplicities shall be reported as E-MODIFIER-CONFLICT
          text:
            An attribute has one ownership (owns, refs, shares) and one
            multiplicity (many, optional). Two of either kind, or one written
            twice, say two things at once: an error at the attribute, which keeps
            the first one written.
          rationale: DS-18, Owner 2026-11-20: what contradicts itself is said, never guessed.
          verification VER-00187-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm two ownerships, two multiplicities or one written twice are E-MODIFIER-CONFLICT, and the first written is kept
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_types_as_text.cpp::TestTypesAsText.TwoOwnershipsOrTwoMultiplicitiesAreAConflict
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_static.cpp::TestStatic.TheOracleFindingsAndNothingElse
          }
        }
        constraint CST-00005-1 {
          tags: language, lld, class
          status: approved
          category: language
          bok: BOK-00004-1
          title: Every modifier shall be a word only in front of a name: nothing is reserved
          text: + abstract: bool is an attribute named abstract.
          rationale: decided 2026-10-26: a code base designed in DriftSpec keeps its names.
        }
        requirement REQ-00028-2 {
          tags: language, lld, class
          status: approved
          title: Ownership and multiplicity shall be written on the attribute, and every name of its type that the design declares shall be related; parameters and returns shall give use
          text:
            owns (default), refs, shares; many is *, optional 0..1, neither 1.
            A type is the code's text (std::map<std::string, Pool*>, list[Step],
            Pool | None): each name in it that the design declares is related
            with the attribute's ownership and multiplicity, or as a use from a
            parameter or a return; ds knows no container of any language. The
            one designed name of a type is what a call through it reaches.
            E-OWNERSHIP-CYCLE.
          rationale:
            R16. Revision 2 (DS-18, Owner 2026-11-20): the multiplicity is
            written, never read from a type whose language ds does not know, and
            W-MODIFIER-ON-VALUE goes: a modifier on what nobody designs is
            incompleteness, not incoherence.
          verification VER-00021-4 {
            title: Integration tests confirm ownership and multiplicity are written on the attribute, every designed name of its type is related, a name qualified with dots as with ::, and parameters and returns give use
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_static.cpp::TestStatic.OwnershipAndMultiplicityComeFromTheAttribute
              tests/integration/ds/render/test_irexport.cpp::TestIrExport.AnEnumIsAValueSoItIsAssociatedNotOwned
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_type_reading.cpp::TestTypeReading.AQualifiedContainerHandsTheCallToItsElement
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_types_as_text.cpp::TestTypesAsText.MultiplicityIsWrittenInFrontOfTheName
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_types_as_text.cpp::TestTypesAsText.ADottedNameRelatesAsTheSameNameWithTwoColons
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00029-2 {
          tags: language, lld, class
          status: approved
          title: A template shall be identified by name and arity and its arguments checked against their bounds, and a base or a method shall carry its template arguments as written
          text:
            class Repository<T: Entity>; E-TEMPLATE-BOUND, never against an
            argument that is the user's own parameter (class Holder<T> : Base<T>).
            A base is written as the code writes it, IFormat<scene::SceneDocument>
            or Box[int]: its first name is what the class inherits and the whole
            text is kept; a method or a function takes parameters of its own,
            each<F>(f: F).
          rationale: R15. Revision 2 (DS-18, 2026-11-20): bases with arguments and templated methods, as the code has them (DS-150).
          verification VER-00022-3 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a template is identified by name and arity and its arguments checked against their bounds, and a base or a method carries its template arguments as written
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_static.cpp::TestStatic.EnumsAndTemplatesHaveIdentities
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_type_reading.cpp::TestTypeReading.ATemplatesArityCountsItsOwnArgumentsOnly
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_types_as_text.cpp::TestTypesAsText.ABaseAndAMethodCarryTheirTemplateArgumentsAsWritten
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00030-1 {
          tags: language, lld, class
          status: approved
          title: Operators shall be declared in the class header as a set and refined in the body
          text: class Vector <<==, ordered, +, []>>; E-OPERATOR-UNDECLARED.
          rationale: R17.
          verification VER-00023-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm operators are declared in the class header as a set and refined in the body
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_static.cpp::TestStatic.OperatorsAreDeclaredInTheHeaderAndRefinedInTheBody
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00031-2 {
          tags: language, lld, class
          status: approved
          title: Abstract and value shall be derived from the members, never written
          rationale:
            What is derived cannot contradict what is declared (R14). A class whose methods are all
            abstract is an abstract class, drawn and inherited as one: interface is only the contract of a
            box (Owner, 2026-11-18, DS-301).
          verification VER-00024-2 {
            title: Integration tests confirm abstract and value are derived from the members, never written
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_static.cpp::TestStatic.AbstractAndValueAreDerived
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00032-2 {
          tags: language, lld, class
          status: approved
          title: Any entity or member shall open a body with fields, notes, annotations and nested artefacts, and a member's body with rules
          rationale:
            R8, R12, R13. Owner, 2026-11-07 (DS-94): the bracket contracts ([requires: ...]) are gone; what
            a member requires of its calls is a rule in its body, one syntax for one thing.
          verification VER-00025-2 {
            title: Integration tests confirm any entity or member opens a body with fields, notes and annotations, and no prose contract is kept
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_static.cpp::TestStatic.NotesLiveInMemberBodies
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_rules.cpp::TestRules.ABracketContractDoesNotParse
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00101-1 {
          tags: language, lld, class
          status: approved
          title: A class shall declare its destructor as ~Name(), and an interface may have one
          text:
            A method named ~ and the class's name is its destructor (flag destructor). It is no
            part of the contract: like a constructor, it does not count against the derived
            interface stereotype, so an interface declares the virtual destructor the C++ guide
            asks of it.
          rationale: Found splitting the checker (DS-138, DS-143): IStep needs a virtual destructor and the language could not say it.
          verification VER-00110-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm ~Name() is the destructor and an interface keeps its stereotype with one
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_destructors.cpp::TestDestructors.ATildeAndTheClassNameIsItsDestructor
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_destructors.cpp::TestDestructors.AnInterfaceKeepsItsDestructorAndStaysAnInterface
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00033-1 {
          tags: language, lld, class
          status: approved
          title: An enum shall be a value type with its values, annotated like a class
          verification VER-00026-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm an enum is a value type with its values, annotated like a class
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/bind/test_packages.cpp::TestPackages.AnEnumRealisesRequirementsLikeAClass
          }
        }
      }

      // ---- function ----
      requirement REQ-00063-1 {
        tags: language, lld, visibility
        status: approved
        title: Any child of a box may be private, and nothing outside its parent shall reach it
        text:
          One rule at every level: a box connects to another through a public child. A package's
          public children are its interfaces and modules, a module's its classes and functions, a
          class's its methods and attributes. A child is public unless it says -, and is seen from
          inside its parent, so siblings see each other. A member that says # is protected: seen
          from inside its class and from a class that derives from it (DS-224). A call or a type
          that crosses anything private or protected on its way down is W-NOT-VISIBLE; a public
          member showing a private type is W-PRIVATE-LEAK.
        rationale: Owner, 2026-11-05. There is no port concept: a port is a public child, and an interface is one that crosses a process. Whether the code respects this is argos's job.
        verification VER-00068-2 {
          title: Integration tests confirm any child of a box may be private, and nothing outside its parent reaches it
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_visibility.cpp::TestVisibility.AnyChildMayBePrivateAndIsPublicUnlessItSaysSo
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_visibility.cpp::TestVisibility.APrivateModuleIsNotReachedFromAnotherBox
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_visibility.cpp::TestVisibility.APrivateClassIsNotReachedFromAnotherModule
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_visibility.cpp::TestVisibility.APrivateMethodIsNotCalledFromAnotherClass
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_visibility.cpp::TestVisibility.APrivateFunctionIsNotCalledFromAnotherBox
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_visibility.cpp::TestVisibility.SiblingsSeeEachOther
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_visibility.cpp::TestVisibility.EveryOtherCrossingIsReportedExactlyAsMarked
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_visibility.cpp::TestVisibility.APublicMemberThatShowsAPrivateTypeIsALeak
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_visibility.cpp::TestVisibilityAlone.APublicOperatorThatShowsAPrivateTypeIsALeak
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_audit_defects.cpp::TestAuditDefects.AProtectedMemberIsHiddenFromAClassThatDoesNotDeriveFromIt
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_inherited_members.cpp::TestInheritedMembers.AProtectedMemberIsSeenFromADerivedClassAndHiddenFromAnyOther
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00054-2 {
        status: approved
        tags: language, lld, function
        verification VER-00058-1 {
          title: Unit tests confirm a function is an operation of its module: the functions oracle
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_functions.cpp::TestFunctions.TheOracleFindingsAndNothingElse
        }
        title: A function shall be an operation of a module, outside any class, identified by its module and its name
        text:
          function name(params) -> type in a module, with a member's optional
          visibility in front, public when absent; two of one name are
          E-DUPLICATE-IDENTITY. A call to a function nobody designs is text
          (REQ-00024-3).
        rationale:
          A Python module or a C++ namespace declares operations no class
          holds; a design that wraps them in a class the code does not have
          drifts from it. Migrating iris (2026-10-26) found 133 of its design
          entities were functions. Revision 2 (DS-18, 2026-11-20): no extern
          module declares them.

        requirement REQ-00055-2 {

          status: approved
          tags: language, lld, function
          title: A function shall carry a method's signature and body
          text: Parameters and the return give use; the body holds notes, annotations, rules and nested artefacts.
          verification VER-00059-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a function carries a method's signature and body
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_functions.cpp::TestFunctions.AFunctionIsAnOperationOfItsModule
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_functions.cpp::TestFunctions.ItsSignatureGivesUseAndItsBodyIsAMethods
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00056-1 {
          status: approved
          tags: language, lld, function
          title: The behaviour of a function shall run on its parameters and locals, and a call with no target shall reach a function of the module
          text:
            sequence gather { }; there is no self. A call with no target is a
            function resolved outward from the module, `-> tasks::gather()` one
            of another module; a method reaches one its class does not declare.
            In a sequence diagram a function runs on its module's lifeline.
          verification VER-00060-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm the behaviour of a function runs on its parameters and locals, and a call with no target reaches a function of the module
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_functions.cpp::TestFunctions.ItsBehaviourRunsOnItsParametersAndCallsItsNeighbours
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_functions.cpp::TestFunctions.ASequenceOfAFunctionRunsOnItsModule
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00057-1 {
          status: approved
          tags: language, lld, function
          title: A class diagram shall draw each function as a box of its own, stereotyped function
          verification VER-00061-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a class diagram draws each function as a box of its own, stereotyped function
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_functions.cpp::TestFunctions.AClassDiagramDrawsEachFunctionAsABox
          }
        }
      }

      // ---- sequence ----
      requirement REQ-00034-1 {
        tags: language, lld, sequence
        status: approved
        verification VER-00054-1 {
          status: approved
          title: Unit tests confirm a sequence is the behaviour of a method: the behaviour oracle
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_behaviour.cpp::TestBehaviour.TheOracleFindings
        }
        title: A sequence shall be the behaviour of a method, identified by it
        rationale: block 3, 2026-10-25.

        requirement REQ-00035-1 {
          tags: language, lld, sequence
          status: approved
          title: A sequence shall be identified by its resolved subject and scenario, wherever it is written
          text: sequence Class::method [/ scenario]; two declarations are a duplicate; the unnamed scenario is the default.
          verification VER-00027-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a sequence is identified by its resolved subject and scenario, wherever it is written
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_behaviour.cpp::TestBehaviour.ABehaviourIsIdentifiedByItsSubject
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00036-2 {
          tags: language, lld, sequence
          status: approved
          title: The participants of a sequence shall be its static context: self, the attributes, the parameters, the locals written and the results of earlier messages
          rationale: Revision 2 adds the results (DS-51): a call on what an earlier message returned is an ordinary design, and it read as an undeclared participant.
          verification VER-00028-6 {
            title: Integration tests confirm the participants of a sequence are its static context: self, the attributes, the parameters, the locals written and the results of earlier messages, scoped as the code scopes them
            status: approved
            text:
              Revision 5 adds how a name resolves when two of them share it (DDEV-1242): a local
              hides a member of its name, a method is no value a message is sent to, and a result
              named like an attribute is stored in that attribute. Revision 6 (DS-18): a name used
              before the message that gives it is text, unchecked.
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_values.cpp::TestNameScopes.ALocalHidesAMemberOfItsNameAndAResultIsStoredInTheAttributeOfItsName
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_values.cpp::TestValues.ALocalInsideAFrameIsALocal
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_behaviour.cpp::TestBehaviour.ParticipantsComeFromTheStaticContext
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_values.cpp::TestValues.AResultIsAParticipantOfTheMessagesAfterIt
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_values.cpp::TestValues.AResultUsedBeforeItsMessageIsText
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_inherited_members.cpp::TestInheritedMembers.ASelfCallReachesAMethodTheClassInherits
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_inherited_members.cpp::TestInheritedMembers.AnInheritedAttributeIsAParticipant
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_inherited_members.cpp::TestInheritedMembers.ACallOnASubclassReachesTheMethodOfItsBase
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_inherited_members.cpp::TestInheritedMembers.APrivateMemberOfTheBaseStaysHidden
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00132-1 {
          tags: language, behaviour, levels
          status: approved
          title: A sequence shall be the behaviour of a use case, a package or a module as well: a scenario written at their cut
          text:
            sequence UC-00001-1 { } is a scenario of the use case, and sequence UC-00001-1 / sold-out
            another; sequence sales { } is what a box does. Its names resolve from the scope the use
            case is declared in, or from inside the box. In a scenario of a use case a message with
            nobody written before its arrow is sent by whoever asks for the use case, from outside the
            design; in one of a box, by the box. A level written alone is a complete design: nothing
            below it has to exist.
          rationale:
            Owner, 2026-11-15 and 16 (DS-232; model audit findings G18, G6 and G9, probes e03 and
            e82): a sequence between packages, another between modules, another between classes, each
            written on its own, without defining every method in between. A calls B and B calls C
            took two sequences and a diagram with depth, so a high-level flow was as many sequences
            as hops. The vision said a module or a package could be a subject and the engine refused it.
          verification VER-00148-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a use case, a package or a module is the subject of a scenario, and that the scenario of a box is drawn between what it names
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_levels.cpp::TestLevels.AUseCaseOrABoxIsTheSubjectOfAScenario
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_levels.cpp::TestLevels.AScenarioOfABoxIsDrawnBetweenWhatItNames
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00133-2 {
          tags: language, behaviour, levels
          status: approved
          title: A participant of a sequence shall be nameable as what it is: a package, a module, an interface or a class the behaviour sees
          text:
            sales in shell -> sales.sell(item) is the package shop@sales, resolved like any name, from
            the innermost scope outward; no local declares it. A value of the static context of that
            name comes first (REQ-00036-2). A name that is neither, the sender's included, is
            text, and its message is not checked (REQ-00024-3). In a drawing a box that is named is one lifeline, the same one
            a closed box has on a cut (REQ-00129-1), and ds query shows answers it.
          rationale:
            Owner, 2026-11-15 (DS-232, probe e80): a box could not be a participant, so a scenario
            between boxes needed an interface in every box and a sequence for every operation on the way.
            Revision 2 (DS-18, 2026-11-20): W-PARTICIPANT-UNDECLARED goes, a name nobody designs being text.
          verification VER-00149-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a package and a module take part in a scenario by being named, and that the query answers them
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_levels.cpp::TestLevels.ABoxTakesPartByBeingNamed
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_levels.cpp::TestLevels.TheQueryShowsWhoTakesPartInAWrittenScenario
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00134-1 {
          tags: language, behaviour, levels
          status: approved
          title: A message shall be sent by the participant written before its arrow, and by the subject of the behaviour when none is written
          text:
            sales -> warehouse.take(item) leaves the sales. The sender is who calls: what it may see
            (W-NOT-VISIBLE) and what it declares to use (W-CONSUMES-UNDECLARED) are checked from it, and
            it is where the message leaves in a drawing. A message with no sender written is the
            subject's, as before.
          rationale:
            Owner, 2026-11-15 (DS-232, probe e80). The grammar kept the sender of a message from the
            proof of concept and the engine ignored it: written in one block, every message left the
            first participant.
          verification VER-00150-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a message leaves the participant written before its arrow, in the model and in the drawing
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_levels.cpp::TestLevels.TheParticipantWrittenBeforeTheArrowSendsTheMessage
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00135-1 {
          tags: language, behaviour, levels, coverage
          status: approved
          title: A message to a box shall be bound to the operation the box offers under that name, and be kept as a label, listed as coverage, when it offers none
          text:
            A package offers the operations of its interfaces, a module its functions. Bound, the
            message is a call like any other: its arguments are checked (E-MSG-ARITY, W-ARG-TYPE), it
            expands to the behaviour of the operation, one step below, and the box stays the
            participant. With no operation of that name, or with more than one, the message is a
            label: the level below is not written yet, which is W-MESSAGE-NO-OPERATION in the coverage
            (REQ-00124-2) and never a finding. A message to an interface or a class that lacks the
            operation is still W-MSG-UNDECLARED: what is written is written.
          rationale:
            Owner, 2026-11-16 (DS-232): write the high level, and when the level below is implemented
            the two are matched. The link needs nothing written: the message names what the box
            offers. A written purchase and its detail lifted onto the same boxes draw the same
            crossings in the same order, which is what DS-233 will check.
          verification VER-00151-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a message to a box is bound to the operation the box offers, that one no operation answers is a label listed as coverage, that what clashes with the level below is a finding, and that the written level and the lifted detail draw the same crossings
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_levels.cpp::TestLevels.AMessageToABoxIsBoundToTheOperationTheBoxOffers
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_levels.cpp::TestLevels.AMessageNoOperationAnswersIsALabelListedAsCoverage
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_levels.cpp::TestLevels.WhatClashesWithTheLevelBelowIsAFinding
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_levels.cpp::TestLevels.TheWrittenLevelAndTheDetailLiftedOntoItsBoxesDrawTheSameCrossings
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00136-1 {
          tags: language, behaviour, levels, coverage
          status: approved
          title: The scenario of a use case and the behaviour below it shall agree on what crosses between the participants of the scenario, and in what order
          text:
            The level below a scenario of a use case is the behaviour of the operation it is asked
            through: a message nobody sends, bound to an operation that has a behaviour (the scenario
            of the same name, when the operation has one). That behaviour is unfolded and lifted onto
            the participants of the scenario: whatever one of them holds, it stands for. The crossings
            the scenario writes have to be found among those of the level below, in order: a message
            bound to an operation finds a call of that operation, a label a crossing between the same
            two. The higher level may leave crossings out. One it writes that the level below never
            makes, or makes fewer times, is W-LEVEL-NO-CROSSING; one made at another place is
            W-LEVEL-ORDER. Both need the sender to be all written below: something is seen to happen
            in it, and every call made in it has a behaviour. Otherwise what the sender does is not
            known yet and the crossing is W-LEVEL-NOT-BELOW in the coverage (REQ-00124-2), which says
            which steps of the high level have their detail beneath. A scenario with no behaviour
            below it is held to nothing.
          rationale:
            Owner, 2026-11-15 and 16 (DS-233, model audit finding G18): with behaviour at every level
            the coherence between the levels can be checked; write the high level and, when the low
            one is implemented, the two are matched. A finding needs two written facts that clash, so
            a level that is not written is never one. On a crossing of the finer level that the
            coarser does not show: no finding, an abstraction omits.
          verification VER-00152-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a scenario and the behaviour below it that cross alike agree, that a crossing never made and two turned around are findings, that one whose sender is not all written is coverage, and that a scenario with nothing below is held to nothing
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_levels.cpp::TestLevels.AScenarioAndTheBehaviourBelowItThatCrossAlikeAgree
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_levels.cpp::TestLevels.ACrossingTheBehaviourBelowNeverMakesAndTwoTurnedAroundAreFindings
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_levels.cpp::TestLevels.ACrossingWhoseSenderIsNotAllWrittenBelowIsCoverage
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_levels.cpp::TestLevels.AScenarioWhoseLevelBelowIsNotWrittenIsHeldToNothing
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00138-1 {
          tags: language, behaviour, levels, relations
          status: approved
          title: A message written between two participants shall say that the sender uses the receiver
          text:
            sales -> warehouse.take(item) gives the relation sends from the sales to the warehouse,
            once, however many messages say it. The sender is the participant written before the
            arrow, or the box a scenario is of; whoever asks for a use case is outside the design
            and gives none. Between boxes it is a use (REQ-00137-1), so a design that has only its
            high level draws its boxes connected by what its scenarios say.
          rationale:
            Owner, 2026-11-16 (DS-230, DS-232). A design with only its high level drew its boxes
            unconnected, though its scenarios said who calls whom: the drawing of the boxes knew
            only the relations of the detail.
          verification VER-00154-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a message written between two participants gives one sends relation from its sender, and that a design with only its high level draws its boxes connected
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_levels.cpp::TestLevels.AMessageWrittenBetweenTwoParticipantsSaysTheSenderUsesTheReceiver
              tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_drawing.cpp::TestWrittenLevel.AMessageWrittenBetweenTwoBoxesConnectsThemInTheDrawingOfTheBoxes
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00059-2 {
          tags: language, lld, sequence
          status: approved
          title: A behaviour shall say what it gives back with return, and that value shall be checked against what its operation declares
          text:
            return takes a name or a literal, never an expression. When the operation
            promises a designed class, a value of another class, or a literal, is
            reported (W-RETURN-TYPE); a return the design does not declare (void, int,
            None) is never compared. A name the context does not know is left alone,
            as an argument is. An operation that declares no return has nothing to
            check against.
          rationale: DS-52. With the scenario a call follows, a trace reaches the value it ends in, which is what lets an interface promise an outcome. Revision 2 (DS-18, 2026-11-20): ds knows no language's types.
          verification VER-00063-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a behaviour says what it gives back with return, and that value is checked against what its operation declares
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_scenarios.cpp::TestScenarios.AReturnIsCheckedAgainstWhatTheOperationDeclares
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_scenarios.cpp::TestScenarios.AReturnThatAgreesIsNotAFinding
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00060-1 {
          tags: language, lld, sequence, interface
          status: approved
          title: An operation of an interface shall have a sequence, and each outcome it promises shall name a scenario whose trace ends in that value
          text:
            sequence shell::buy / refused goes down through the scenario each call names.
            An operation with no sequence is W-OPERATION-NO-BEHAVIOUR, an outcome naming no
            scenario W-SCENARIO-UNDECLARED, and one whose trace ends in another value
            W-OUTCOME-MISMATCH. What cannot be told, a value that is not a literal at the end
            of the trace, gives no finding.
          rationale: DS-54. This is the link from the high level design to the low level one, and what keeps a promise such as exit 2 true.
          verification VER-00065-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm an operation of an interface has a sequence, and each outcome it promises names a scenario whose trace ends in that value
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_interfaces.cpp::TestInterfaces.AnOperationIsTheSubjectOfItsSequenceAndItsTraceGoesDown
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_interfaces.cpp::TestInterfaces.AnOperationWithNoSequenceIsAFinding
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_interfaces.cpp::TestInterfaces.AnOutcomeThatNamesNoScenarioIsAFinding
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_interfaces.cpp::TestInterfaces.AnOutcomeIsCheckedAgainstTheValueItsTraceEndsIn
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_interfaces.cpp::TestInterfaces.TheSequenceOfAnOperationIsDrawnDownThroughItsScenario
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00061-1 {
          tags: language, lld, state, interface
          status: approved
          title: The state machine of an interface shall be its navigation, with views as its states, and shall report the views with no way in or out
          text:
            state web { view Home { text: ... } Home -> Done on buy }. A view holds free text and
            the requirements and constraints that bind it, nothing that reinvents HTML. An event
            that names an operation goes down to it; any other is a label. W-VIEW-UNDECLARED,
            W-VIEW-UNREACHABLE and W-VIEW-NO-WAY-OUT. Views are optional: a bare machine asks nothing.
          rationale: Owner, 2026-11-05. In a graphical interface the navigation is the design; the look is a requirement or a constraint.
          verification VER-00066-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm the state machine of an interface is its navigation, with views as its states, and reports the views with no way in or out
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_interfaces.cpp::TestInterfaces.AViewIsAStateWithItsTextAndWhatBindsIt
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_interfaces.cpp::TestInterfaces.AnEventThatNamesAnOperationGoesDownAndAnyOtherIsALabel
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_interfaces.cpp::TestInterfaces.TheNavigationReportsViewsWithNoWayInOrOut
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00065-1 {
          tags: language, lld, sequence
          status: approved
          title: A branch of a sequence shall name a scenario, a path through that one behaviour, so what scenarios share is written once
          text:
            opt <prose> / refused { ... return 2 } names a branch; a bare return ends it. The path of a
            scenario takes what is shared and its own branch, up to the return that ends it; the default
            path takes no named branch; of an alt with one named side, the other side is taken; a branch it
            cannot tell is kept as a frame. A call, a diagram and an exit code ask for the path by its
            name, as they do for a sequence of its own, which stays for paths that share nothing. A name
            given twice is W-SCENARIO-TWICE. A behaviour asked for by its subject is drawn whole, its early
            returns included; an unnamed call follows the default path.
          rationale: Owner, 2026-11-05. Repeating the opening of every scenario was the largest cost of the design of ds, and a maintenance risk. checkMessage went from twelve sequences to one.
          verification VER-00070-2 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a branch of a sequence names a scenario, a path through that one behaviour, so what scenarios share is written once
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_branches.cpp::TestBranches.ADiagramThatAsksForAScenarioNobodyDeclaresIsAFinding
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_branches.cpp::TestBranches.ABranchNamesAScenarioAndABareReturnEndsIt
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_branches.cpp::TestBranches.APathTakesWhatIsSharedAndItsBranchUpToItsReturn
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_branches.cpp::TestBranches.TheDefaultPathTakesNoNamedBranch
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_branches.cpp::TestBranches.ACallThatNamesABranchEntersThatPath
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_branches.cpp::TestBranches.AnExitCodeIsCheckedAgainstTheEndOfItsPath
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_branches.cpp::TestBranches.ANameGivenTwiceIsAFinding
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_branches.cpp::TestBranches.ABehaviourAskedForByItsSubjectIsDrawnWholeWithItsEarlyReturns
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_branches.cpp::TestBranches.AScenarioIsDrawnAsItsPathWithoutTheBranchesItSkips
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_branches.cpp::TestBranches.AnUnnamedCallFollowsTheDefaultPath
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00110-1 {
          tags: language, lld, sequence, failure
          status: approved
          title: A sequence shall say where a path ends failing and where a failure of what it calls is handled, whatever the mechanism
          text:
            fail ParseError ends a path where a return would go, with the failure's name as text, as a
            type is written. attempt { ... } on IoError / unreadable { ... } runs its steps and handles
            what they fail with, one on each: a handler that fails with another translates the failure,
            one that returns resolves it, and a failure no handler names goes on to the caller. The
            default path does not fail: it takes the attempt's steps and no handler; a handler that
            names a scenario is the path of that failure. The diagram draws the failure and each handler.
          rationale: Owner, 2026-11-13 (DS-180, DS-187). One model of failure for exceptions, error values and error codes; what escapes each operation is computed from where failures start and where they are handled (DS-188).
          verification VER-00126-2 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a path ends failing with its failure's name, an attempt runs on every path and a handler only on the path that names it, and the diagram draws both
            status: approved
            text: Revision 2 (DS-189) holds the example to reporting only the failures its signatures misstate, now that it has some.
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_failures.cpp::TestFailures.TheExampleReportsNothingButTheFailuresItsSignaturesMisstate
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_failures.cpp::TestFailures.APathThatFailsEndsThereWithTheFailuresName
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_failures.cpp::TestFailures.AnAttemptRunsOnEveryPathAndAHandlerOnlyOnThePathThatNamesIt
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_failures.cpp::TestFailures.AHandlerKeepsTheFailureItHandles
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_failures.cpp::TestFailures.TheSequenceDiagramDrawsTheFailureAndTheHandlers
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00111-1 {
          tags: language, lld, sequence, failure, check, query
          status: approved
          title: The checker shall compute what failures escape each operation, with the path each comes up by, and the query shall answer it
          text:
            A failure starts where a path ends failing and goes up to every caller that does not handle
            it: an attempt handles, around the steps it runs, what its handlers name; a handler's own
            steps are not handled by its attempt. Computed until nothing changes, so calls that call
            each other end. Each operation carries escapes, the failures, and escape.<failure>, the
            operations it comes up through and the behaviour (or the scenario of one) that starts it.
            A call ds cannot follow, to an operation with no behaviour, is taken not to fail.
            ds query <workspace> failures <operation> answers each escaping failure, named by the
            operation it starts in.
          rationale: Owner, 2026-11-13 (DS-180, DS-188). The design writes only where a failure starts and where it is handled; what escapes is inferred, for exceptions, error values and codes alike.
          verification VER-00127-2 {
            title: Tests confirm a failure escapes every caller that does not handle it, with its path and the scenario it starts in, that a handler's own failure goes on, that calls calling each other end, and that the query answers it
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_failures.cpp::TestFailures.AFailureEscapesEveryCallerThatDoesNotHandleIt
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_failures.cpp::TestFailures.EachEscapeKeepsThePathItComesUpByAndTheScenarioItStartsIn
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_failures.cpp::TestFailures.TheQueryAnswersWhatEscapesAnOperationByWhereEachStarts
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_failure_escapes.cpp::TestFailureEscapes.AFailureInsideAnAttemptThatHandlesItEscapesNothing
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_failure_escapes.cpp::TestFailureEscapes.AHandlersOwnFailureIsNotHandledByTheAttemptThatHoldsIt
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_failure_escapes.cpp::TestFailureEscapes.CallsThatCallEachOtherEndWithWhatEitherLetsEscape
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00112-1 {
          tags: language, lld, failure, check
          status: approved
          title: A signature shall say what an operation may fail with, and the checker shall hold it to what escapes, and a boundary to saying it
          text:
            + load(path: string) -> Book fails LoadError, ParseError promises what may escape; fails
            nothing promises none. What escapes and the signature does not say is W-FAILURE-UNDECLARED;
            what it says and no path of its behaviour fails with is W-FAILURE-UNREACHED. An interface
            operation is a boundary: what escapes it is said in its signature, or nothing may. Any other
            operation is held to its signature only when it writes one. An operation with no behaviour
            fails as its signature says, so a call into a library can be said to fail.
          rationale: Owner, 2026-11-13 (DS-180, DS-189). noexcept is the promise that nothing escapes, decided per operation by the design, not by a rule over every method.
          verification VER-00128-1 {
            title: Tests confirm a signature is held to what escapes, a boundary says what escapes it, and an operation with no behaviour fails as its signature says
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_failures.cpp::TestFailurePromises.WhatEscapesAndTheSignatureDoesNotSayIsAFinding
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_failures.cpp::TestFailurePromises.WhatTheSignatureSaysAndNoPathFailsWithIsAFinding
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_failures.cpp::TestFailurePromises.AnOperationWithNoBehaviourFailsAsItsSignatureSays
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_failures.cpp::TestFailurePromises.ABoundarySaysWhatEscapesItOrNothingMay
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00123-1 {
          tags: language, lld, failure, check
          status: approved
          title: A handler of a failure that no step of its attempt can fail with shall be reported as W-HANDLER-UNREACHED
          text:
            The failures the steps of an attempt can raise are those its messages fail with and those
            that escape the operations they call. A handler that names none of them handles nothing.
            Said only when every operation called in the attempt says how it fails, by a behaviour or by
            a signature; a call that says nothing leaves the handler alone.
          rationale:
            Model audit 2026-11, finding G17 (DS-224): on IoErorr handled nothing and was reported only
            when the operation happened to promise what it fails with. The failures corpus handled Busy,
            which no step raised; its read now says it may fail with Busy.
          verification VER-00139-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a handler of a failure no step raises is reported and the one that handles a raised failure is not
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_audit_defects.cpp::TestAuditDefects.AHandlerOfAFailureNoStepRaisesHandlesNothing
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00058-3 {
          tags: language, lld, sequence
          status: approved
          title: A value of a sequence shall have the type of what produced it, and its uses shall be checked against that type
          text:
            The result of a message has the type its operation returns. A call on a
            result is checked as a call on any other participant, and an argument
            against its parameter (W-ARG-TYPE): a value of a designed class must
            derive from the designed class the parameter names, and a literal, a
            number or a string, fits no designed class. A type the design does not
            declare (int, std::string, None) is the code's word, which ds does not
            read: it is never compared, and the result of any operation may be
            taken. A value whose type is not known, because its operation declares
            no return or does not resolve, has nothing to be checked against and
            gives no finding.
          rationale: DS-51; revision 2 adds literal arguments (DS-64): the code passes them, and naming them invented values. Static and dynamic joined only by name and arity, so a sequence could not say anything about what flows through it. Revision 3 (DS-18, Owner 2026-11-20): ds knows no language, so the families of literals and W-RESULT-NONE go; what needs no table of a language stays.
          verification VER-00062-3 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a value of a sequence has the type of what produced it, and its uses are checked against that type
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_values.cpp::TestValues.ALiteralDiffersOnlyFromADesignedClass
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_values.cpp::TestValues.ACallOnAResultIsCheckedAgainstItsType
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_values.cpp::TestValues.AValueOfUnknownTypeHasNothingToBeCheckedAgainst
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_values.cpp::TestValues.AnArgumentOfTheWrongTypeIsReported
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_values.cpp::TestValues.ADerivedClassSatisfiesItsBaseAsAnArgument
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_values.cpp::TestValues.ATemplateWithCommasIsOneParameter
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_values.cpp::TestValues.TheResultOfAnyOperationMayBeTaken
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_types_as_text.cpp::TestTypesAsText.ALiteralDiffersOnlyFromADesignedClass
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00037-1 {
          tags: language, lld, sequence
          status: approved
          title: A message shall name an operation of its target's type and match its arity, inside loop, alt and opt frames with prose conditions
          verification VER-00029-2 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a message names an operation of its target's type and matches its arity, inside loop, alt and opt frames with prose conditions, each step held by what encloses it
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_behaviour.cpp::TestBehaviour.AnAltHoldsItsSidesAsBranchesAndEachSideItsSteps
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_checker.cpp::TestChecker.MessagesToExpectedTypesAreNotReportedTwice
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_audit_defects.cpp::TestAuditDefects.ASlashFollowedByTextStaysInTheProseOfACondition
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00108-1 {
          tags: language, lld, sequence
          status: approved
          title: A message shall call a class's static operation by its qualified name, with no instance
          text:
            `-> diff::Diff::compare(a, b)`: a message with no participant whose qualified name
            names an operation of a class calls it on the class itself, as `-> tasks::gather(x)`
            calls a function of another module. The operation must be static; one that is not
            is W-MSG-NOT-STATIC, since it needs an instance: a local, an attribute or a parameter.
          rationale:
            DS-60 (Owner, 2026-11-13): Diff::compare is static, and a sequence could only call
            through a participant, so the design declared a local the code does not have.
          verification VER-00124-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a method's and a function's behaviour call a static operation on its class, and one that is not static is reported
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_static_calls.cpp::TestStaticCalls.AMethodsBehaviourCallsAStaticOperationOnItsClass
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_static_calls.cpp::TestStaticCalls.AFunctionsBehaviourCallsAStaticOperationOnItsClass
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_static_calls.cpp::TestStaticCalls.AnOperationThatIsNotStaticNeedsAnInstance
          }
        }
        requirement REQ-00038-2 {
          tags: language, lld, sequence
          status: approved
          title: A message to an operation with a behaviour shall expand to its default scenario or to the one it names, and coupling to another module shall be derived
          text:
            A call names a scenario as a declaration does, with a slash, and a scenario
            the operation does not declare is W-SCENARIO-UNDECLARED. Revision 2 adds the
            naming (DS-52): a trace could only go down through the default scenario.
          verification VER-00030-2 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a message to an operation with a behaviour expands to its default scenario or to the one it names, and coupling to another module is derived
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_scenarios.cpp::TestScenarios.ACallThatNamesAScenarioExpandsToThatOne
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_scenarios.cpp::TestScenarios.ACallThatNamesNoScenarioExpandsToTheDefault
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_scenarios.cpp::TestScenarios.AScenarioNobodyDeclaresIsAFinding
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_behaviour.cpp::TestBehaviour.AMessageToAnOperationWithABehaviourExpandsToIt
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_behaviour.cpp::TestBehaviour.CouplingIsDerivedFromWhatABehaviourTouchesOutsideItsModule
          }
        }
      }

      // ---- state ----
      requirement REQ-00039-1 {
        tags: language, lld, state
        status: approved
        verification VER-00055-1 {
          status: approved
          title: Unit tests confirm a state machine is the behaviour of a class
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_behaviour.cpp::TestBehaviour.StateEventsAndActionsAreOperations
        }
        title: A state machine shall be the behaviour of a class
        rationale: block 3, 2026-10-25.

        requirement REQ-00040-1 {
          tags: language, lld, state
          status: approved
          title: The events and actions of a transition shall be operations of the class, and the guard shall be prose
          text: Idle -> Running on run [guard] / report; no nested states until a design asks for them.
          verification VER-00031-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm the events and actions of a transition are operations of the class, and the guard is prose
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_behaviour.cpp::TestBehaviour.StateEventsAndActionsAreOperations
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_checker.cpp::TestChecker.AStateEventMustBeAnOperationOfItsEntity
          }
        }

        requirement REQ-00109-2 {
          tags: language, lld, state, check
          status: approved
          title: The checker shall report a state of a class's machine that no transition leads to from its initial state
          text:
            A state named in the transitions that the walk from the initial state never enters is
            W-STATE-UNREACHABLE: a state left over, or a transition missing. The initial state is
            the one written, or the one the machine starts in when it is not written (REQ-00143-1).
            A machine with neither says nothing, since where it starts cannot be told. The
            navigation of an interface has its own (W-VIEW-UNREACHABLE).
          rationale:
            DS-53, measured 2026-11-05: `Lost -> Idle on go`, never entered, passed silently.
            Revised 2026-11-17 (DS-253): the start of a machine whose states have rules is derived
            and no longer written, and leaving it out turned this check off in silence.
          verification VER-00125-2 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a state nothing leads to from the initial one, written or derived, is reported, and a machine fully reached or whose start cannot be told says nothing
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_behaviour.cpp::TestStateReach.AStateNothingLeadsToFromTheInitialOneIsAFinding
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_behaviour.cpp::TestStateReach.AMachineEveryStateOfWhichIsReachedOrWithNoInitialStateSaysNothing
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_behaviour.cpp::TestStateReach.TheWalkStartsWhereTheMachineStartsWhenItsStartIsNotWritten
          }
        }

        requirement REQ-00232-1 {
          tags: language, lld, state
          status: approved
          title: A state machine shall say the states it ends in
          text:
            `final <State>, ...` inside a state block names the states the machine ends in; every
            final line adds to them. A final state is a state of the machine like any other, so one
            nothing leads to is W-STATE-UNREACHABLE (REQ-00109-2). A state nothing leaves is not
            taken as final: a machine written in part says only what it says.
          rationale: DS-442, the Owner, 2026-11-24, on a side-by-side mock; the word is his.
          verification VER-00258-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a machine keeps the final states its final lines name, in order, and they are among its states
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_states.cpp::TestStates.AMachineKeepsItsFinalStatesInOrderAmongItsStates
          }
        }

        requirement REQ-00233-1 {
          tags: language, lld, state, check
          status: approved
          title: The checker shall report a transition out of a state the machine says is final
          text:
            A transition whose source is a final state is W-STATE-LEAVES-FINAL, said where the
            machine is written: the machine cannot both end there and go on. A state with no way out
            that is not final says nothing.
          rationale: DS-442. A final state is a promise about the machine; a transition out of it is a contradiction, and a contradiction is what the checker says.
          verification VER-00259-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a transition out of a final state is a finding, once, and a state with no way out is not taken as final
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_states.cpp::TestStates.ATransitionOutOfAFinalStateIsAFindingAndAStateWithNoWayOutIsNotTakenAsFinal
          }
        }

        requirement REQ-00140-1 {
          tags: language, lld, state, logic
          status: approved
          title: A state of a machine shall be written with the rule that computes it over what its subject holds
          text:
            Paid if status = Paid, not cancelled. A state is computed, not kept: its rule says when the
            subject of the machine is in it, over what the subject holds. The literals are joined by
            commas and all hold. A literal is an attribute compared with a value (=, !=), or a name
            alone: a boolean attribute, or a condition with a name when the subject holds nothing so
            called; `not` turns a literal round. A state may have several rules, each one way of being
            in it. The jumps are written beside the rules as they always were (REQ-00040-1), and a
            state with no rule is a name, as before.
          rationale:
            Owner, 2026-11-16 and 17 (DS-249). The states of a machine are a layer above the structure
            and the behaviour: neither has to name them, and real code keeps its state in several
            places. The author writes the machine whole, and what he writes is then held to the rest.
          verification VER-00156-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a state is written with the rule that computes it, over an enum value, a boolean and a difference, that a state takes several rules and that a name the subject does not hold is a condition
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_states.cpp::TestStates.AStateIsWrittenWithTheRuleThatComputesItOverWhatItsSubjectHolds
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_states.cpp::TestStates.SeveralRulesOfOneStateAndAConditionWithANameAreNoFinding
          }
        }

        requirement REQ-00141-1 {
          tags: language, lld, state, logic, check
          status: approved
          title: The checker shall report two states of a machine that can hold at once, with a context in which both do
          text:
            The rules of a machine exclude each other, so that whatever its subject holds it is in one
            state. Two rules of different states that some context satisfies together are
            W-STATE-OVERLAP, and the finding says one such context: Lamps can be Left and Both at once:
            when hazard = true, lever = Left. It is read from the rules alone: two rules exclude each
            other when they ask one name for values that cannot both be. Two rules of one state may
            hold together. What no rule covers is nothing to report: a machine that says less is
            complete.
          rationale:
            Owner, 2026-11-17 (DS-249): at any moment the machine is computed again and falls into one
            state. Measured on a sketch: rules read in order, with one that takes the rest, hide every
            context their author did not think of; rules that each stand alone show them.
          verification VER-00157-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm two states that can hold at once are a finding that says a context in which both do
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_states.cpp::TestStates.TwoStatesThatCanHoldAtOnceAreAFindingThatSaysAContextInWhichTheyDo
          }
        }

        requirement REQ-00142-1 {
          tags: language, lld, state, logic, check
          status: approved
          title: The checker shall report a rule of a state that cannot hold as it is written
          text:
            A comparison with a value the type of the attribute does not have is W-STATE-VALUE, and it
            says the values the type has. A comparison of something the subject does not hold is
            W-STATE-UNDECLARED. A rule whose literals exclude each other is W-STATE-IMPOSSIBLE: the
            subject can never be in that state. A rule reported so is said once and is not held to the
            others. An attribute whose values cannot be listed (a number, a text) is compared as
            written.
          rationale:
            DS-249. Probe s01 of the model audit: an enum of Open, Paid and Sent beside a machine of
            Open, Paid and Shipped passed in silence.
          verification VER-00158-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a value its type lacks, an attribute the subject lacks and a rule that excludes itself are findings, each said once
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_states.cpp::TestStates.AValueItsTypeLacksAnAttributeTheSubjectLacksAndARuleThatExcludesItselfAreFindings
          }
        }

        requirement REQ-00143-1 {
          tags: language, lld, state, logic
          status: approved
          title: A machine shall start in the state the defaults of its subject are in, when its start is not written
          text:
            The start of a machine whose states have rules is derived: the state in which the defaults
            of the attributes its rules read are. A start that is written is the start. Defaults that
            are in no state, and an attribute with no default, give no start.
          rationale: DS-249. Derived things are never written (vision 3): the defaults already say where the subject starts.
          verification VER-00159-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a machine with no start written starts in the state its subject's defaults are in
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/render/test_state_view.cpp::TestStateView.AMachineWithNoStartWrittenStartsWhereTheDefaultsOfItsSubjectAre
          }
        }

        requirement REQ-00145-1 {
          tags: language, lld, state, logic, check
          status: approved
          title: The checker shall report a call of an event that the machine of its receiver lets happen from no state the receiver may be in
          text:
            The behaviours written are read as they are, and nothing is asked of them. Along a
            behaviour, what it calls an event on may be in any state to begin with; a call of an
            event moves it by the jumps on that event, and a jump with a guard may leave it where it
            was. A frame may not happen: what comes after it finds the receiver as the frame leaves it
            or as it was; the sides of an alt are each one way, and a loop is read once, since its
            receiver may be another each time. An operation that is no event of the machine moves
            nothing. A call the machine lets happen from none of the states its receiver may be in is
            W-STATE-ORDER, where the call is made: Desk::rush calls order.cancel() when order can only
            be Shipped: the machine of Order lets cancel happen from Open or Paid.
          rationale:
            Owner, 2026-11-17 (DS-250): the author writes the machine whole, and the tool looks for
            where each of its events is called. From a state not every other can be reached, so the order of
            the calls is what a behaviour can contradict.
          verification VER-00161-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a call out of the order of the machine is a finding where it is made, and that calls in order, a call after a frame that may not happen and a first call are not
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_states.cpp::TestStates.ACallOfAnEventTheMachineLetsHappenFromNoStateItsReceiverMayBeInIsAFindingWhereItIsMade
              tests/integration/ds/check/test_states.cpp::TestStates.CallsInTheOrderOfTheMachineACallAfterAFrameThatMayNotHappenAndAFirstCallAreNoFinding
          }
        }
      }

    }

    // ======================= logic ========================================
    requirement REQ-00095-2 {
      tags: language, logic, rules
      status: approved
      title: The language shall declare rules and definitions in the box they apply to, in a formal syntax: what they speak about, which of them, and what holds, answering to a requirement
      text:
        rule <name> { @requirement <ID>; for <kind> <variable> [in <box>], ...; where <literals>;
        holds <literals> | never <literals> } in a package, a module, a class or a member;
        definition <name>(<parameters>) { if <literals> ... } names a relation derived from others,
        one case per if line. A literal is a relation of the metamodel (c uses x), a kind (x is
        class), containment (x in p@core), a comparison (=, !=, <, <=, >, >=) of entities or values,
        a definition applied (reaches(c, x)) or the literal argument of a call (arg(k, msg, v)),
        negated by not; a bare name is a variable, an entity is written qualified and a value is a
        number or a string. What a rule
        says is read from this syntax and never from prose: holds: <words> does not parse. A rule
        is a design piece with a name, not an artefact with a revision: it makes a requirement real
        in a narrower context and may name the entities of the design, which a requirement does not.
        A rule that answers to no requirement, or says no claim or more than one, and a definition
        with no case, are findings.
      rationale:
        Owner, 2026-11-07 (DS-94): the logic perspective of a design, beside its structure and its
        behaviour; constraints that spoke about the design and design rules hidden in comments get a
        place where they can be checked.
      verification VER-00104-2 {
        title: Integration tests confirm rules and definitions are declared in the box they apply to, link to their requirement, and the ones that say nothing are reported
        status: approved
        method: test
        tests:
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rules.cpp::TestRules.ARuleIsADesignPieceInTheBoxItAppliesTo
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rules.cpp::TestRules.ADefinitionIsARelationWithTheCasesInWhichItHolds
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rules.cpp::TestRules.ARuleThatAnswersToNothingOrClaimsNothingIsReported
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rules.cpp::TestRules.ProseIsNotARule
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rules.cpp::TestRules.ARuleOfAMemberIsNamedUnderIt
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_audit_defects.cpp::TestAuditDefects.ARuleOfAnAttributeIsNamedUnderTheAttribute
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rules.cpp::TestRules.RuleAndDefinitionStayNamesWhereANameIsExpected
      }
    }

    requirement REQ-00117-1 {
      tags: language, logic, rules, check
      status: approved
      title: A relation literal that binds an end to a kind the relation never joins there shall be reported as W-RULE-NEVER-MATCHES
      text:
        The kind of a variable is read from its range or from a positive kind literal, and the kind
        of an entity from the model. When a relation literal binds its source to a kind the relation
        never leaves, or its target to a kind it never reaches, by the metamodel, the literal matches
        nothing: the rule is decided by vacuity. It is a warning at the literal; the rule is still
        evaluated.
      rationale:
        Model audit 2026-11, finding G1 (DS-213): for class c ... never c uses x could never be
        broken, since uses leaves members and never a class, and it was the example of ds manual
        rule. A vacuous rule stands as the verification of a requirement and is always green. The
        manual's example now speaks about the members.
      verification VER-00133-2 {
        status: approved
        title: Integration tests confirm a literal that can never match is reported, a rule reading uses off an attribute among them, and the member-level rule is broken where the design breaks it
        method: test
        tests:
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rules.cpp::TestRules.ALiteralThatCanNeverMatchIsReported
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rules.cpp::TestRules.ARuleReadingUsesOffAnAttributeIsToldItMatchesNothingAndRefsSeesTheField
      }
    }

    requirement REQ-00096-1 {
      tags: language, logic, rules, check
      status: approved
      title: The checker shall decide every structural rule over the model and report each binding of its ranges that breaks it
      text:
        Rules are evaluated as Datalog with stratified negation over the model: the facts are the
        declared entities with their kinds, every relation of the metamodel and containment at any
        depth. A definition is computed to its fixpoint, recursion included; a rule's holds is broken
        by each binding of its ranges and where that has no answer, its never by each binding that
        has one, reported with the binding and the answer (E-RULE-BROKEN). The model is finite, so
        every rule is decided. A formula that names an unknown kind, relation, entity or definition
        (E-RULE-UNKNOWN), applies a definition to the wrong number of arguments (E-RULE-ARITY),
        tests a variable no positive literal binds (E-RULE-UNSAFE) or negates a definition through
        its own recursion (E-RULE-UNSTRATIFIED) is reported and not evaluated.
      rationale:
        Owner, 2026-11-07 (DS-94): structural rules are always decidable in the design, in a formal
        or semi-formal syntax; nothing is inferred from prose or keywords. Rules about behaviour wait
        for contracts and recorded runs.
      verification VER-00105-4 {
        title: Integration tests confirm rules are decided over the model, broken bindings are reported with their answer, whichever argument of a definition is bound and in whatever order its stratum is fixed, malformed or undecidable formulas are rejected, and the rules of a file that stops parsing are decided to the end
        status: approved
        method: test
        tests:
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rule_evaluation.cpp::TestRuleEvaluation.ARuleIsBrokenByEveryBindingThatBreaksIt
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rule_evaluation.cpp::TestRuleEvaluation.ANegationTestsWhatIsAlreadyBound
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rule_evaluation.cpp::TestRuleEvaluation.ADefinitionRecursUntilItsFixpoint
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rule_evaluation.cpp::TestRuleEvaluation.ADefinitionAnswersWhicheverOfItsArgumentsIsBound
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rule_evaluation.cpp::TestRuleEvaluation.ADefinitionThatAppliesAnotherIsFixedAfterIt
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rule_evaluation.cpp::TestRuleEvaluation.WhatARuleNamesMustExistAndItsVariablesBeBound
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rule_evaluation.cpp::TestRuleEvaluation.ANegationThroughRecursionIsRejected
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_half_typed.cpp::TestHalfTypedFile.TheRulesOfAFileThatStopsParsingAreDecidedAndTheCheckEnds
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_half_typed.cpp::TestHalfTypedFile.TheSameRulesAreDecidedOnceTheFileParses
      }
    }

    requirement REQ-00196-1 {
      tags: language, logic, rules, check
      status: approved
      @refines SREQ-00012-1
      title: A rule shall read what an entity is as name(x), id(x) and kind(x), match a value against a pattern, and read a member's flag with is
      text:
        name(x), id(x) and kind(x) are what the entity bound to x is: its name as written, its identity and the name of
        its kind. They are the language's, so no definition takes those names (E-RULE-RESERVED), and no kind declares a
        field named name or id; the dot stays for a field the design writes, as r.status. A term of either kind reads
        what its variable is bound to and never binds it. `name(c) matches "^I[A-Z]"` holds when the value contains a
        match of the pattern, an ECMAScript regular expression; each pattern is compiled once, when its formula is
        validated, and a pattern that is no string or does not compile is reported at the literal and its formula is
        not evaluated (E-RULE-PATTERN). `m is abstract` holds when m is bound to an entity that carries the flag, a
        flag some kind declares; like a match, it tests what is bound and binds nothing.
      rationale:
        Owner, 2026-11-24 (DS-402, DS-403): the design rules the Owner uses in every design (an interface named I and a
        capital, an abstract class A and a capital, a factory ending in Factory) need what a class is called, a pattern
        and whether a member is abstract. The syntax is the Owner's.
      verification VER-00216-1 {
        status: approved
        title: Integration tests confirm a rule reads an entity's name, identity and kind, matches a value against a pattern compiled once, reads a member's flag, and is told when its pattern is no string or does not compile, a definition takes a reserved name or a property is all that binds a variable
        method: test
        tests:
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rule_properties.cpp::TestRuleProperties.ARuleReadsTheNameTheIdentityAndTheKindOfAnEntity
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rule_properties.cpp::TestRuleProperties.ANameMatchesAPattern
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rule_properties.cpp::TestRuleProperties.AMembersFlagIsReadWithIs
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rule_properties.cpp::TestRuleProperties.APatternThatIsNoStringOrDoesNotCompileIsReported
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rule_properties.cpp::TestRuleProperties.ADefinitionThatTakesAReservedNameIsReported
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rule_properties.cpp::TestRuleProperties.APropertyNeverBindsItsVariable
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rule_properties.cpp::TestRuleProperties.NameAndIdAreNoFieldAKindDeclares
      }
    }

    requirement REQ-00097-1 {
      tags: language, logic, rules, check, sequence
      status: approved
      title: The checker shall decide the rules over the calls of the sequences against the literal arguments they pass, and report each call that breaks one where the call is written
      text:
        A call of a sequence is a message entity that calls an operation; arg(k, p, v) holds when
        the call k passes to the parameter p of what it calls a value v written as a literal in the
        sequence, a number or a string. Values compare numbers as numbers and anything else as
        written. A call that passes a name instead of a literal has no arg fact for that parameter,
        so a rule that asks for one in its where leaves it out: undecided in the design, it is
        neither kept nor broken. A broken rule is reported at the first thing it ranges over, the
        call in its sequence.
      rationale:
        Owner, 2026-11-07 (DS-94): contracts are rules too, with the rule's syntax; brackets gone,
        one syntax for one thing.
      verification VER-00106-1 {
        title: Integration tests confirm rules over calls are decided against their literal arguments, calls passing names are left out, and the finding sits on the call
        status: approved
        method: test
        tests:
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rule_evaluation.cpp::TestRuleEvaluation.ARuleOfAMemberIsKeptOrBrokenByTheLiteralsItsCallsPass
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rule_evaluation.cpp::TestRuleEvaluation.ValuesCompareNumbersAsNumbersAndTheRestAsWritten
      }
    }

    requirement REQ-00098-1 {
      tags: logic, rules, check, export
      status: approved
      title: The engine shall keep every binding that breaks a rule, with its answer and where it lands, and hand it structured to the export and to the Python package
      text:
        Each breach the evaluator finds is kept by the rule's identity: the variables of its ranges
        and where as bound, for a never the answer that breaks it, and the location of the first
        thing the rule ranges over. All of them are kept, although the findings stop at a few per
        rule. The JSON export carries them under breaches, and the Python package loads them as Model.breaches, so
        a reader lights what breaks a rule without reading the findings' prose.
      rationale:
        Owner, 2026-11-09 (DS-108): the rules must be seen to be understood; the prototype read the
        findings' messages, which is no contract.
      verification VER-00107-1 {
        title: Tests confirm every breach is kept with its bindings, its answer and where it lands, and travels structured through the export and the Python package
        status: approved
        method: test
        tests:
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_rule_evaluation.cpp::TestRuleEvaluation.EveryBreachIsKeptWithItsBindingsItsAnswerAndWhereItLands
          tests/integration/python/test_driftspec.py::TestRules::test_what_breaks_a_rule_comes_with_its_bindings_and_its_answer
      }
    }

    // ======================= artefacts ====================================
    requirement REQ-00042-1 {
      tags: language, artefacts
      status: approved
      verification VER-00056-1 {
        status: approved
        title: Unit tests confirm artefacts are held in the same model: the artefacts oracle
        method: test
        tests:
          tests/integration/ds/check/test_artefacts.cpp::TestArtefacts.TheOracleFindingsAndNothingElse
      }
      title: The language shall hold requirements, constraints and verifications as artefacts of the same model
      rationale: block 1, 2026-10-25.

      requirement REQ-00043-1 {
        tags: language, artefacts
        status: approved
        title: An artefact shall be identified by its id with its revision
        text: REQ-00001-2: the revision is part of the identity; a link to a revision no longer declared is W-REVISION-DRIFT, resolved to the declared one.
        verification VER-00032-2 {
          title: Integration tests confirm an artefact is identified by its id with its revision
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_artefacts.cpp::TestArtefacts.TheRevisionIsPartOfTheIdentityAndLinksNameIt
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_usecase_links.cpp::TestUseCaseLinks.ARealisationBehindTheRevisionIsReportedAndFollowsIt
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_usecase_links.cpp::TestUseCaseLinks.ASpecialisationThatNamesNoRevisionIsUnpinned
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_usecase_links.cpp::TestUseCaseLinksAlone.AUseCaseNobodyDeclaresIsSaidOnce
        }
        constraint CST-00006-1 {
          tags: language, artefacts
          status: approved
          category: language
          bok: BOK-00004-1
          title: Every link to an artefact shall name a revision
          text: An unpinned link is E-REF-UNPINNED.
          rationale: Pinning links to a revision is what lets the tool tell who stayed behind when an artefact moved (block 1).
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00125-1 {
        tags: language, artefacts
        status: approved
        title: Nesting an artefact in a design entity shall say what annotating it says, and whatever may nest an artefact may annotate it
        text:
          A requirement nested in a class, a member, a function or another design entity binds
          requires from the entity, and a nested constraint binds constrained-by, as @requirement
          and @constraint do: two syntaxes that only change where the artefact is filed. Every kind
          that may nest an artefact, an attribute and an operator among them, may carry either
          annotation, and satisfies and constrained-by leave the same kinds, a state machine
          included. In a package or a module a nested artefact still gives its level.
        rationale:
          Owner, 2026-11-15, on the model audit (findings I7 and I6, DS-217): nesting and annotating
          mean exactly the same. A requirement nested in a class bound about, which no step read, so
          the class answered to nothing. Whether a requirement written in a box also means the box
          answers to it is left to the Owner.
        verification VER-00141-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm nesting in a design entity gives satisfies or constrained-by, an attribute annotates as it nests, and a box keeps the level
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_static.cpp::TestNesting.NestingAnArtefactInADesignEntityIsAnnotatingIt
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_static.cpp::TestStatic.ConstraintsAreArtefactsInBothForms
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00044-1 {
        tags: language, artefacts
        status: approved
        title: Nesting shall give the relation between artefacts, and annotations shall add relations from elsewhere
        text: a requirement in a requirement refines it; a verification in a requirement verifies it; @refines, @requirement, @constraint elsewhere.
        verification VER-00033-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm nesting gives the relation between artefacts, and annotations add relations from elsewhere
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/bind/test_binder.cpp::TestBinder.NestingGivesTheRelation
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_artefacts.cpp::TestArtefacts.LevelByPlacementAndNestingGivesTheRelation
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_artefacts.cpp::TestArtefacts.TheSeparateFileIsTheNormalPlace
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00114-1 {
        tags: language, artefacts, annotations
        status: approved
        title: An annotation word the language gives no meaning to shall be reported as W-ANNOTATION-UNKNOWN
        text:
          A @word outside the table of annotations is a warning where it is written,
          naming the word and the words the language knows. The link it makes is kept
          (refers): the warning does not drop it, and loading never fails on it.
        rationale:
          Model audit 2026-11, finding G2 (DS-210): @requirment REQ-1 traced nothing and
          reported nothing, the easiest silent failure of the language and in its
          traceability. No design of the fleet uses a free annotation word.
        verification VER-00130-1 {
          status: approved
          title: Integration tests confirm an unknown annotation word is reported where it is written and its link is kept, and the artefacts corpus marks one
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/bind/test_binder.cpp::TestBinder.AnAnnotationWordTheLanguageDoesNotKnowIsReportedAndKept
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_artefacts.cpp::TestArtefacts.TheOracleFindingsAndNothingElse
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00118-1 {
        tags: language, artefacts, static
        status: approved
        title: A cycle of refines or of inherits shall be an error, and a requirement that refines one written inside its own box shall be reported as W-REFINES-UPWARD
        text:
          Requirements that refine each other through any chain are E-REFINES-CYCLE; classes or
          use cases that derive from each other are E-INHERITANCE-CYCLE; each cycle is said once.
          A requirement whose refined requirement is written inside its own box, further down the
          containment axis, is a warning: the detail stands above what it details. The axis is
          read from where each is written, never from the prefix of its id.
        rationale:
          Model audit 2026-11, finding G10 (DS-214): only the ownership cycle was reported. A
          cycle is a contradiction between written things, not an absence. Reporting a
          requirement that nothing answers to stays out on purpose: that is absence (DS-216).
        verification VER-00134-1 {
          status: approved
          title: Integration tests confirm refines and inherits cycles are errors, a tree is not, and an upward refinement is reported while a downward one is not
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_cycles.cpp::TestCycles.RequirementsThatRefineEachOtherAreACycleAndTheUpwardOneIsSaid
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_cycles.cpp::TestCycles.ClassesThatDeriveFromEachOtherAreACycleAndATreeIsNot
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_cycles.cpp::TestCycles.ARequirementThatRefinesOneOfTheSystemIsNoFinding
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00168-1 {
        tags: language, artefacts, static
        status: approved
        title: A piece that answers to a requirement whose home does not hold it shall be reported as W-SATISFIES-OUTSIDE-HOME, naming the box that holds the home and every piece that realises it
        text:
          A requirement written in a box has that box as its home. A box, interface, class,
          function or member that answers to it from outside that box is a warning: the
          requirement is written at one place and realised outside it. The finding names the
          innermost box around the home that holds every piece realising the requirement: where
          the requirement would stand with all of them inside. What holds a piece is read from
          where it is written, never from the prefix of its id. A requirement written at the root
          holds everything, and a requirement nothing realises is no finding. A system that
          adopts the requirement of another, written in a package of its own that no box shares
          with it (a library's), answers to it as to any artefact: no finding either.
        rationale:
          Owner, 2026-11-19 and 2026-11-20 (DDEV-1460, DDEV-1470): a requirement two modules
          realise is the requirement of the package that holds them both, and the coherence of
          the design is the language's to check, forwarded to argos by its plugin; argos crosses
          the design with the code. The nesting and the annotation are two written facts that
          clash, not an absence.
        verification VER-00185-1 {
          status: approved
          title: Integration tests confirm a piece outside its requirement's home is reported with the box that holds them all, and a piece inside the home, a requirement at the root or one another system adopts is not
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_satisfies_home.cpp::TestSatisfiesHome.APieceOutsideTheHomeIsReportedWithTheBoxThatHoldsThemAll
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_satisfies_home.cpp::TestSatisfiesHome.APieceInsideTheHomeARequirementAtTheRootOrOneAnotherSystemAdoptsIsNoFinding
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00124-2 {
        tags: language, check, coverage
        status: approved
        title: What is not yet written shall be no finding, kept apart as coverage and answered when asked
        text:
          Eight codes say that something is not yet written: an interface operation with no
          sequence (W-OPERATION-NO-BEHAVIOUR), an outcome with no scenario
          (W-USECASE-OUTCOME-UNHANDLED), a use case nothing realises (W-USECASE-UNREALISED), an
          approved requirement nothing verifies (W-REQ-NO-VERIFICATION), an approved requirement
          nothing satisfies, by itself or through every requirement that refines it
          (W-REQ-UNREALISED), an approved constraint nothing verifies (W-CST-NO-VERIFICATION), a
          verification by test with no tests (W-VER-NO-TEST), a rule that answers to no requirement
          (W-RULE-NO-REQUIREMENT). A draft owes none of them. They are of severity coverage: ds check does not report or
          count them, the check's JSON and the export carry them apart, and ds query <workspace>
          coverage [identity] lists them. An oracle corpus marks them with // ? as it marks a
          finding with // !.
        rationale:
          Owner, 2026-11-15, on the model audit (finding I12, DS-216): the mechanism punishes
          information that is not consistent, never information that is missing. A finding needs
          two written facts that clash. A design with only its high level drew five warnings and no
          contradiction. Where else coverage is asked (argos's report) is left open. Revision 2
          (Owner, 2026-11-24, DS-429, DS-430): first readers of the manual took a clean check for a
          finished design; an approved requirement nothing satisfies and an approved constraint
          nothing verifies are coverage too, as the intent chapter says DriftSpec tells.
        verification VER-00140-2 {
          title: Integration and system tests confirm the eight absences are kept apart as coverage, not reported by the check, and every corpus means exactly the coverage it marks
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_artefacts.cpp::TestArtefacts.TheOracleFindingsAndNothingElse
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_coverage.cpp::TestCoverage.AnAbsenceIsNoFindingAndTheQueryAnswersIt
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_coverage.cpp::TestCoverage.AnApprovedRequirementNothingRealisesAndAConstraintNothingVerifiesAreCoverage
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00045-1 {
        tags: language, artefacts
        status: approved
        title: An artefact shall carry title, text, rationale, status and tags; a verification its method and tests; a constraint its category and bok
        text: One-line forms for a requirement and a constraint; draft when no status.
        verification VER-00034-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm an artefact carries title, text, rationale, status and tags; a verification its method and tests; a constraint its category and bok
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_artefacts.cpp::TestArtefacts.FieldsAsDecided
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_static.cpp::TestStatic.ConstraintsAreArtefactsInBothForms
        }
      }
    }

    // ======================= diagrams =====================================
    requirement REQ-00047-1 {
      tags: language, diagrams
      status: approved
      verification VER-00057-1 {
        status: approved
        title: Unit tests confirm a diagram is a selection of the model
        method: test
        tests:
          tests/integration/ds/render/test_requirements_view.cpp::TestRequirementsView.ThePackageTreeWithoutDesign
          tests/integration/ds/render/test_text.cpp::TestText.DrawsEveryBoxWithItsNameAndMembers
      }
      title: A diagram shall be a selection of the model that decides what to print and never what to draw
      rationale: Keeping the views as selections keeps them from drifting.

      requirement REQ-00048-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams
        status: approved
        title: A diagram shall select by id, module or package, with include and exclude, and print by hide and show
        verification VER-00036-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a diagram selects by id, module or package, with include and exclude, and prints by hide and show
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_irexport.cpp::TestIrExport.ListsTheDeclaredDiagrams
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_irexport.cpp::TestIrExport.IncludeSelectsAndTheModuleBecomesAContainer
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00116-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams
        status: approved
        title: A diagram pattern shall read the same in every kind of diagram, and one that names what nothing declares shall be reported as W-DIAGRAM-UNDECLARED
        text:
          include, exclude and open take an identity, a prefix with * over the identity or
          the scope (p@m::* is the module p@m and everything in it), a module named as such,
          or a template written without its arity, read alike by class, sequence, state and
          requirement diagrams. A pattern that names nothing the workspace declares is a
          warning at the diagram. A pattern over a box that exists and is empty is not: a
          level with no children is valid.
        rationale:
          Model audit 2026-11, findings G3 and I9 (DS-212): a diagram whose include named a
          class that had been moved drew less and said nothing, while the same broken
          reference in the model is a finding; p@m::* selected a module in a class diagram and
          nothing in a requirement diagram; package:x, documented, selected nothing anywhere.
          The check found two such diagrams in driftspec's own design.
        verification VER-00132-1 {
          status: approved
          title: Integration tests confirm a pattern that names nothing is reported and an empty box is not, and a requirement diagram reads a module pattern as a class diagram does
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_open_boxes.cpp::TestOpenBoxes.APatternThatNamesWhatNothingDeclaresIsAFinding
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_open_boxes.cpp::TestDiagramPatterns.ARequirementDiagramReadsAModulePatternAsAClassDiagramDoes
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00049-3 {
        tags: language, diagrams
        status: approved
        title: A sequence diagram shall select a behaviour as it is written and expand by depth
        text:
          Revision 3 (DS-231): it no longer lifts to a level asked for with level: module;
          it stands on the cut the diagram writes (REQ-00129-1), and the calls it follows
          are said with unfold:, fold: and depth: (REQ-00130-1).
        verification VER-00037-8 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a sequence diagram selects a behaviour as it is written and expands by depth
          status: approved
          text:
            Revision 6 (DS-337): a depth too large for an int is the finding of a depth that is no
            number, at the same place, and is drawn as one; it no longer stops ds. The filters read
            a neighbours: too large for an int the same way.
            Revision 7 (DS-362): the check of a depth is the check of a neighbours: too, which, as a
            word or too large for an int, is W-DIAGRAM-NEIGHBOURS at the diagram and draws no
            neighbours; and a depth past what the calls reach draws as *, at once.
            Revision 8 (DS-385): the depth and the neighbours are read by Filters, which names the
            filter of each kind, so the test that reads a number too large is TestFilters'.
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_values.cpp::TestValues.TwoObjectsOfOneTypeAreTwoLifelines
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_values.cpp::TestValues.AFrameWithNoMessageIsStillDrawn
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_open_boxes.cpp::TestOpenBoxes.DepthStarIsEveryLevelAndNeverAnAbort
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_open_boxes.cpp::TestOpenBoxes.ADepthThatIsNoNumberIsAFindingAndStillDraws
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_open_boxes.cpp::TestDepthTooLarge.ADepthTooLargeForAnIntIsTheFindingOfADepthThatIsNoNumber
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_open_boxes.cpp::TestDepthTooLarge.ADepthTooLargeForAnIntDrawsAsADepthThatIsNoNumberAndNeverAnAbort
            tests/integration/ds/query/test_diagram_filters.cpp::TestFilters.ANumberTooLargeForAnIntIsReadAsNoNumberAndNeverAnAbort
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_open_boxes.cpp::TestNeighboursPastTheReach.NeighboursThatAreNoNumberAreAFindingAtTheDiagramAsADepthIs
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_open_boxes.cpp::TestDepthPastTheReach.ADepthPastTheCallsDrawsAsEveryLevelAndEndsPromptly
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_scenarios.cpp::TestScenarios.ASubjectAloneDrawsItsDefaultScenario
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_scenarios.cpp::TestScenarios.AScenarioIsSelectedAsItIsWrittenAndItsTraceFollowsIt
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_scenarios.cpp::TestScenarios.AStarSelectsEveryScenario
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_scenarios.cpp::TestScenarios.TheArrowBackCarriesWhatTheBehaviourReturns
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_behaviour.cpp::TestBehaviour.ASequenceDiagramExpandsByDepthAndLinksAtDepthZero
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00129-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, cut
        status: approved
        title: A sequence diagram shall stand on the cut the diagram writes: one lifeline for each closed box, and the messages that cross from one to another
        text:
          The open:, close: and cut: of a class diagram mean the same in a sequence diagram. Every
          participant is drawn as what stands for it on the cut: a closed box is one lifeline for
          everything inside it, and what no closed box holds keeps a lifeline of its own. A message
          whose two ends have the same representative is internal and is not drawn; one that
          crosses is drawn between the representatives, and into a closed box it says which of its
          children takes it (Desk::sell). A frame of the detail is kept around what crosses inside
          it, and one in which nothing crosses is not drawn. ds query shows answers the same
          participants. It replaces level: module, which lifted to one fixed rung, drew only the
          direct calls and ignored depth; a diagram that still writes it is told so
          (W-FIELD-UNKNOWN).
        rationale:
          Owner, 2026-11-16 (DS-229, DS-231), model audit finding I13. The sketch showed the same
          purchase on ten lifelines with every class, on six with the sales open, and on eight at
          the modules, the conditions of the detail still framing what crosses; driftspec's own
          check went from twenty lifelines to eight. A high-level view of behaviour is the detail
          lifted, so the two cannot disagree.
        verification VER-00145-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a sequence diagram draws one lifeline for each closed box and only the messages that cross, keeps the frames around them, takes the cut of another diagram, and is asked with the same cut
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_sequences.cpp::TestCutSequences.WithNoCutEveryObjectIsALifelineAndDepthSaysHowFar
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_sequences.cpp::TestCutSequences.EachClosedBoxIsOneLifelineForEverythingInsideIt
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_sequences.cpp::TestCutSequences.AMessageInsideAClosedBoxIsNotDrawnAndOneThatCrossesSaysWhoTakesIt
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_sequences.cpp::TestCutSequences.AFrameIsKeptAroundWhatCrossesInsideIt
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_sequences.cpp::TestCutSequences.TheCutRunsAtAKindOfBoxAndWhatIsAboveItStaysOpen
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_sequences.cpp::TestCutSequences.ASequenceTakesTheCutOfTheBoxesDrawnAndStandsOnIt
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_sequences.cpp::TestCutSequences.TheQueryShowsWhoTakesPartOnTheCut
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_behaviour.cpp::TestBehaviour.ADiagramThatClosesTheModulesKeepsOnlyWhatCrossesThem
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00130-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, cut
        status: approved
        title: A sequence diagram shall follow the calls that unfold:, fold: and depth: say, and every call when it stands on a cut and they say nothing
        text:
          The calls have a cut of their own, written like the cut of the boxes. fold: names the
          calls that are not followed (* for every call, a box for every call that lands in it, an
          operation), and every other call is followed. unfold: names the calls that are followed,
          whatever fold: says; written alone it folds every other call, and with nothing after it
          the diagram draws only what the behaviour itself writes. depth: n is the uniform case
          for the calls neither names. With none of the three, a diagram that writes a cut follows
          every call, since what a closed box does inside is seen only through what crosses out of
          it, and a diagram that writes no cut follows none, as before. A pattern that names
          nothing is W-DIAGRAM-UNDECLARED.
        rationale:
          Owner, 2026-11-16 (DS-229, DS-231): depth defined formally. depth: n counts calls and is
          as rigid as a numbered level; the calls worth following are named by where they land.
          The sketch drew a cut at the modules with nothing unfolded and it showed almost nothing,
          which is why a cut follows every call unless told otherwise.
        verification VER-00146-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a sequence diagram follows the calls unfold: names, stops at those fold: names, lets unfold: go through fold:, keeps depth: as the uniform case and reports a pattern that names nothing
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_sequences.cpp::TestCutSequences.UnfoldAloneFoldsEveryOtherCall
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_sequences.cpp::TestCutSequences.FoldStopsAtWhatItNamesAndEveryOtherCallIsFollowed
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_sequences.cpp::TestCutSequences.UnfoldGoesThroughWhatFoldFolds
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_sequences.cpp::TestCutSequences.DepthIsTheUniformCaseOnACutToo
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_sequences.cpp::TestCutSequences.ACallPatternThatNamesNothingAndTheRetiredLevelAreReported
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00131-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, cut, artefacts
        status: approved
        title: A requirement diagram shall draw what answers to a requirement as the box that stands for it on the cut the diagram writes
        text:
          With close: module, a requirement a class answers to is drawn realised by the module
          that holds the class, once. The artefacts themselves are never lifted. ds query shows
          answers the same boxes.
        rationale:
          Model audit finding G9 (DS-231): a requirement diagram had no cut at all, so the trace
          from a requirement of the system could only be read down to the classes.
        verification VER-00147-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a requirement diagram draws what answers to a requirement as the box that stands for it, and is asked with the same cut
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_sequences.cpp::TestCutSequences.WhatAnswersToARequirementIsTheBoxThatStandsForIt
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00139-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, cut, behaviour
        status: approved
        title: A tour shall draw, in one diagram and on one cut, the boxes that take part in a behaviour and the sequence between them
        text:
          diagram TOUR_Buy { kind: tour; include: shop@shell::buy; close: module } takes the fields
          of a sequence diagram: the behaviour it follows, the cut, and the calls it follows. It is
          one drawing of two parts. The sequence, as a sequence diagram of the same fields draws
          it. And who takes part, drawn as boxes: each lifeline of the sequence is one box of the
          other part, whether the cut closes it or a scenario written between boxes names it, with
          the relations between them lifted (REQ-00137-1) and the boxes around them as open
          context. The parts are drawn side by side, and one under the other where the width asked
          for does not hold them, or in a terminal. ds query shows answers who takes part.
        rationale:
          Owner, 2026-11-16 (DS-245). A sequence says who talks to whom and a drawing of boxes says
          how they are held and related; reading one against the other by eye, when each is cut its
          own way, was confusing. One diagram relates the two, so that a step of the behaviour can
          be shown on the boxes it joins: the playground plays it.
        verification VER-00155-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a tour is one drawing of two parts on one cut, each lifeline of its sequence one box of the other part with the relations between them, drawn side by side or one under the other, and asked by who takes part
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_tour.cpp::TestTour.ATourIsOneDrawingOfTwoPartsTheBoxesAndTheSequence
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_tour.cpp::TestTour.EachLifelineOfTheSequenceIsOneBoxOfTheOtherPart
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_tour.cpp::TestTour.TheBoxesAreJoinedByTheRelationsBetweenThem
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_tour.cpp::TestTour.TheSequencePartIsTheSequenceDiagramOfTheSameFields
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_tour.cpp::TestTour.ABoxNamedInAScenarioTakesPartAsOneBoxWithNoCutWritten
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_tour.cpp::TestTour.TheQueryShowsWhoTakesPart
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_tour.cpp::TestTour.ItsPartsAreDrawnSideBySideAndOneUnderTheOtherWhenTheWidthAsksForIt
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_tour.cpp::TestTour.InATerminalTheBoxesComeAboveTheSequence
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00120-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, hld
        status: approved
        title: A class diagram shall draw every box it includes, whatever the box holds
        text:
          A package below the system or a module that a pattern takes is drawn as a box, with or
          without children, and an entity is drawn inside the box its scope names, a package scope
          included: an interface declared in a package sits in that package. Nothing below a box is
          needed to see the box.
        rationale:
          Model audit 2026-11, finding I3 (DS-218), and the Owner's decision of 2026-11-15: every
          abstraction level stands on its own. A design that had only its high level drew two loose
          interfaces and no box, and a package with nothing inside did not appear.
        verification VER-00136-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a box with nothing inside is drawn and an interface sits in the package that declares it
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_open_boxes.cpp::TestEveryBox.ABoxIsDrawnWithOrWithoutChildren
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00121-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, dependencies
        status: approved
        title: A class diagram shall draw a behaviour's dependencies from its subject, count behaviour with or without open, and draw a declared @consumes
        text:
          What a sequence uses or couples to is drawn from its subject's box (a method's class, a
          function, an operation's interface), not from the module it is written in. Behaviour that
          crosses boxes is drawn whether or not the diagram writes open:, so a diagram that does not
          write open: draws what one that opens every box draws; a coupling an edge between the
          two already says gives no second arrow. A box's @consumes of an interface is drawn as a
          dependency, before any behaviour confirms it.
        rationale:
          Model audit 2026-11, finding I2 (DS-219): one dependency was drawn three ways. Without
          open: the arrow left the module; with open: a second arrow appeared; a declared @consumes was
          drawn by the viewer and by no diagram. The single rule for lifting relations is DS-230.
        verification VER-00137-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm one dependency is drawn alike with or without open, from the subject of its behaviour, and a declared use is drawn
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_open_boxes.cpp::TestDependencyDrawing.OneDependencyIsDrawnAlikeWithOrWithoutOpenAndADeclaredUseIsDrawn
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00064-5 {
        tags: language, diagrams, visibility
        status: approved
        title: A class diagram shall open the boxes it names and close the rest, each closed box showing only its public children
        text:
          open: shop@store@stock opens that box and the way to it; open: written alone closes every
          other box, and open: with nothing after it closes them all; a diagram that writes no cut
          draws everything open. An edge that loses an end to a closed box is lifted to it, and what
          stays inside a closed box is not drawn. A behaviour that crosses boxes counts too, so an
          interface reaches what handles it though no type says so. The component view is derived,
          never written.
          Revision 3 (DS-229): open: is one of the ways a cut is written (REQ-00126-1,
          REQ-00127-1, REQ-00128-1); alone it means what it meant.
          Revision 4 (DS-230): a lifted edge no longer says only that one box depends on the other;
          it is a structural bond or a use (REQ-00137-1).
          Revision 5 (DS-244): a closed box lists what it offers as a class lists its members, a
          compartment for each kind of thing in one order (packages, modules, interfaces, classes,
          dataclasses, enums, functions), each by name and each marked with the badge a box of
          its own would carry.
        rationale: Owner, 2026-11-05. Encapsulation has to be seen: which box holds what, and what each one offers.
        verification VER-00069-4 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a class diagram opens the boxes it names and closes the rest, each closed box showing only its public children
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_open_boxes.cpp::TestOpenBoxesBehaviour.ABehaviourThatCrossesBoxesIsAnEdgeOfTheClosedDrawing
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_open_boxes.cpp::TestOpenBoxes.OpenAloneClosesEveryBox
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_open_boxes.cpp::TestOpenBoxes.AClosedBoxShowsOnlyItsPublicChildren
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_drawing.cpp::TestCutDrawing.AClosedBoxListsWhatItOffersByKindEachMarkedWithWhatItIs
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_open_boxes.cpp::TestOpenBoxes.EdgesLiftToTheBoxesThatAreSeenAndOnlyOnceForEachBond
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_open_boxes.cpp::TestOpenBoxes.OpeningABoxShowsTheWayToItAndClosesTheRest
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_open_boxes.cpp::TestOpenBoxes.AnEdgeReachesInsideAnOpenBoxFromAClosedOne
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_open_boxes.cpp::TestOpenBoxes.ADiagramThatDoesNotSayOpenDrawsEverythingOpen
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00126-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, cut
        status: approved
        title: A diagram shall close the boxes close: names, by name, by prefix or by kind, and leave every other box open
        text:
          close: shop@warehouse closes that box; close: shop@sales@* every box that begins so;
          close: module every module wherever it is, and close: package every package below the
          outermost one, so a cut runs at one kind of box without counting levels. What a closed box
          holds is lifted to it, as with open:. The outermost package is the drawing itself and
          never closes. The drawing and ds query shows read the same cut. A pattern that names no
          box is W-DIAGRAM-UNDECLARED; a kind names no box and is none.
        rationale:
          Owner, 2026-11-16 (DS-229), on a sketch run over a shop and over driftspec's own design.
          The abstraction axis is elastic (vision 2b.5): a cut is written as what closes and what
          opens, never as its frontier, so whatever is written is a cut, one branch may go down to
          its classes while another stays one box, and nothing has to be written to make it valid.
        verification VER-00142-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a diagram closes the boxes close: names by name or by kind, lifts what they hold, and is asked with the same cut it is drawn with
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_drawing.cpp::TestCutDrawing.CloseClosesTheBoxItNamesAndTheRestStaysOpen
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_drawing.cpp::TestCutDrawing.AnEdgeThatLosesAnEndToAClosedBoxIsLiftedToIt
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_drawing.cpp::TestCutDrawing.AKindClosesTheBoxesOfThatKindWhereverTheyAre
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_drawing.cpp::TestCutDrawing.ACloseThatNamesNoBoxIsAFindingAndAKindIsNone
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_drawing.cpp::TestCutDrawing.TheQueryShowsWhatTheDrawingDrawsUnderEveryCut
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00127-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, cut
        status: approved
        title: A box that open: names shall stay open, with the way to it, whatever close: closes
        text:
          With both written, close: draws the line and open: goes through it: close: module with
          open: shop@sales@till closes every module but the till. One rule for the two fields, with
          no order between patterns to learn. open: written alone keeps its meaning (REQ-00064-3).
        rationale:
          Owner, 2026-11-16 (DS-229). The sketch first let the pattern that named a box more closely
          win, and a kind leave the rest open; that was a rule to learn, and the Owner asked for a
          cut that is easy to understand.
        verification VER-00143-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm open: goes through what close: closes, and that open: alone still closes every other box
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_drawing.cpp::TestCutDrawing.OpenGoesThroughWhatCloseCloses
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_drawing.cpp::TestCutDrawing.OpenAloneStillClosesEveryOtherBox
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00128-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, cut
        status: approved
        title: A diagram shall take the cut of the diagram cut: names and add what it writes itself
        text:
          cut: HLD_Till takes the open: and close: of that diagram, and what the diagram writes
          itself is added to them, so several drawings share one cut written once. A diagram named
          that nothing declares is W-DIAGRAM-UNDECLARED, and the diagram is drawn with what it
          writes itself. Diagrams that take each other's cut are read once each.
        rationale:
          Owner, 2026-11-16 (DS-229): is a cut declared once and cited, or written by each diagram.
          Both: each diagram writes its cut and another may take it, with no new kind of artefact;
          a cut with a name of its own waits for the open core.
        verification VER-00144-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a diagram draws the cut it takes from another with its own added, and that a cut taken from a diagram nobody declares is reported
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_drawing.cpp::TestCutDrawing.ADiagramDrawsTheCutItTakesFromAnotherAndAddsItsOwn
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_drawing.cpp::TestCutDrawing.ACutTakenFromADiagramNobodyDeclaresIsAFindingAndStillDraws
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00137-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, cut, relations
        status: approved
        title: A relation lifted onto a closed box shall read as one of two bonds, structural or uses, with one arrow of each at most between two boxes
        text:
          Between classes a relation keeps its own kind: composition, aggregation, inheritance,
          realisation, use. When an end of it is lost to a closed box it is lifted, and between
          boxes it is one of two things. A structural bond: one owns, refers to, derives from or
          realises something of the other; drawn as a solid arrow. A use: it names a type of the
          other, calls it, sends it a message or declares its use; drawn as a dashed arrow. Each
          is drawn once between two boxes, however many relations of the detail make it, and what
          stays inside a closed box is not drawn. With the boxes open nothing changes.
        rationale:
          Owner, 2026-11-15 and 16 (DS-230, model audit findings I2 and G1). Relations are never
          merged: using is not composing, aggregating or inheriting, and a closed box turned them
          all into one dependency. Asked which kinds to keep once lifted: use and structural,
          nothing more. A reference a class keeps to something of another box is structural.
        verification VER-00153-2 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a relation lifted onto closed boxes is a structural bond or a use, drawn once each, and that with the boxes open every relation keeps its own kind
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_drawing.cpp::TestCutDrawing.ARelationLiftedOntoClosedBoxesIsOneOfTwoBondsAndOneArrowOfEachAtMost
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_drawing.cpp::TestCutDrawing.WithTheBoxesOpenEveryRelationKeepsItsOwnKind
            tests/integration/ds/check/test_levels.cpp::TestLevels.AUseSaidByAScenarioAndByTheDetailBelowIsDrawnOnceBetweenTwoBoxes
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00155-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, cut, relations
        status: approved
        title: A use drawn between two boxes shall say the interfaces of the one it reaches that the other goes through, with the operations asked of each
        text:
          What a box consumes, uses or calls of an interface of another box is said by the use
          drawn between the two, lifted or written between them as they are: a line per interface,
          its name and the operations asked of it. A use that goes through no interface says
          nothing, and a structural bond never does. The drawing carries it as what the arrow says
          when pointed at.
        rationale:
          Owner, 2026-11-17 (DS-266): in the HLD, resting on a box lights its relations and says
          which interface each one uses. The arrow between two closed boxes is a summary; this is
          what it is made of, read from the model and never drawn by hand.
        verification VER-00171-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a use between two closed boxes says the interface it goes through and the operation asked, and that a use through none says nothing
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_cut_drawing.cpp::TestCutDrawing.AUseBetweenTwoBoxesSaysTheInterfacesItGoesThrough
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00062-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, state
        status: approved
        title: A state diagram shall draw the states and the jumps of a machine, and of an interface its navigation with what each view shows
        text:
          diagram ST_Web { kind: state; include: shop@web }. It reads from the initial state down;
          a jump is an edge with its event and guard, a jump to the same state is a loop, and a view
          shows its text and what binds it. A view no jump names is still drawn.
        rationale: DS-5. In a graphical interface the navigation is the design, so it has to be seen.
        verification VER-00067-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a state diagram draws the states and the jumps of a machine, and of an interface its navigation with what each view shows
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_state_view.cpp::TestStateView.TheViewsAreBoxesWithWhatTheyShowAndWhatBindsThem
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_state_view.cpp::TestStateView.TheJumpsAreEdgesWithTheirEventAndGuard
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_state_view.cpp::TestStateView.TheMachineStartsFromItsInitialState
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_state_view.cpp::TestStateView.AViewNoJumpNamesIsStillDrawn
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_state_view.cpp::TestStateView.TheMachineOfAClassIsDrawnFromItsTransitionsAlone
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_state_view.cpp::TestSelfLoop.ARelationOfABoxWithItselfIsRoutedOutOfItsSideAndBack
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00144-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, state
        status: approved
        title: A state diagram shall draw each state with the rule that computes it
        text:
          A state a rule computes shows its rule under its name, one line for each rule it has, and
          is drawn though no jump names it. The arrow from the start goes to the state the machine
          starts in (REQ-00143-1).
        rationale: DS-249. The rule is what the state means, so it is read where the state is.
        verification VER-00160-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a state diagram draws each state with the rule that computes it, one line for each rule, and a state no jump names
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_state_view.cpp::TestStateView.AStateIsDrawnWithTheRuleThatComputesIt
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_state_view.cpp::TestStateView.AStateWithNoJumpIsStillDrawnAndOneWithTwoRulesShowsBoth
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00234-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, state
        status: approved
        title: A state diagram shall draw the start of its machine as a filled dot, its final states with an arrow to a bullseye, and its states as capsules
        text:
          The start is UML's filled dot with an arrow to the state the machine starts in (the box
          that said start is gone); each final state has an arrow to a bullseye of its own; a state
          is a capsule, rounded where it has sections, with its name in the middle and no badge. In
          the render-IR they are the kinds initial, final and state. The terminal draws ●, ◉ and
          rounded boxes.
        rationale: DS-442, the Owner, 2026-11-24, on a side-by-side mock: "there is no comparison".
        verification VER-00260-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a state diagram starts at a dot, ends at a bullseye for each final state and draws its states as capsules
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_state_view.cpp::TestStateView.TheMachineStartsAtADotAndEndsAtABullseyeForEachFinalStateAndItsStatesAreCapsules
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00251-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, state
        status: approved
        title: A state diagram shall draw its transitions as smooth curves, a transition back to the same state as a loop beside it
        text:
          Every render-IR document says the kind of its diagram, and the drawing routes by it: a state diagram's
          transitions are curves through the points the layering leaves, leaving and entering each state where its
          border faces the next point; two between the same states bow apart; a loop goes out beside the right side of
          its state and back, one outside the other; each label lies on its curve, moved off any state it would fall
          in. The gaps between layers need no lanes. Every other kind keeps its orthogonal routes, and the terminal
          draws every kind orthogonally.
        rationale: DS-443, the Owner, 2026-11-24, on the same side-by-side mock as DS-442. What a document is is said, not guessed from its nodes (code-62, 2026-11-25).
        verification VER-00271-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a state diagram says its kind and is routed in curves with every route labelled where its router put it, while a diagram of any other kind or a drawing not routed by its kind stays orthogonal
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_state_view.cpp::TestStateView.TheTransitionsAreCurvesAndALoopSitsBesideItsStateWhileOtherDiagramsStayOrthogonal
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00146-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, state
        status: approved
        title: A state diagram shall say, on each jump, the behaviours that call its event
        text:
          The jump keeps its label as it is written, and says besides the behaviours written that
          call its event: pay is called in Desk::rush, Desk::sell. A jump whose event no behaviour
          calls says nothing more.
        rationale: DS-250. The machine is written apart from the behaviours; the drawing is where the two are seen together.
        verification VER-00162-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a jump of a state diagram says the behaviours that call its event, and that one nobody calls says nothing more
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_state_view.cpp::TestStateView.AJumpSaysTheBehavioursThatCallItsEventAndOneNobodyCallsSaysNothing
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00147-2 {
        tags: language, diagrams, state
        status: approved
        title: A table diagram shall draw a machine as a table: its events down, its states across, and in each cell where the event takes the state
        text:
          diagram TB_Order { kind: table; include: shop@order::Order }. The events and the states
          are in the order the machine writes them. A cell says the state the jumps on the event
          lead to from the state above it, with the guard of a jump that has one and every jump when
          there are several; where the machine has no jump the cell is empty. It is drawn as SVG and
          as terminal text, like every diagram.
        rationale:
          Owner, 2026-11-16 and 17 (DS-251): the machine seen as a matrix, the states across and the
          operations down. Named table, not matrix (Owner, 2026-11-17, DS-254): the more general word,
          for a table may later cross other things (requirements, constraints, rules). The state diagram shows the ways through; the table shows what is not
          there: every pair of an event and a state the machine says nothing of.
        verification VER-00163-2 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a table diagram is a table of the events of a machine down and its states across with where each event takes each state, drawn as SVG and as text, and that one that names no machine says so
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_table_view.cpp::TestTableView.ATableDiagramIsATableOfTheEventsOfAMachineDownItsStatesAcrossAndWhereEachEventTakesEachState
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_table_view.cpp::TestTableView.TheTableIsDrawnAsSvgAndAsTextWithTheCellAgainstTheMachineMarked
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_table_view.cpp::TestTableView.ATableThatNamesNoMachineSaysSo
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00148-2 {
        tags: language, diagrams, state
        status: approved
        title: A table diagram shall say who calls each event, and the cell in which a behaviour calls one against the machine
        text:
          The heading of a row says the behaviours written that call its event. A call the machine
          lets happen from no state its receiver may be in (REQ-00145-1) is said in the cell of each
          of those states, with the behaviour that makes it: the place in the table where the
          behaviours and the machine disagree.
        rationale: DS-251. The finding says the call; the table shows which jump the author of one of the two left out.
        verification VER-00164-2 {
          title: Integration tests confirm the table says who calls each event and marks the cell in which a behaviour calls one against the machine
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_table_view.cpp::TestTableView.TheTableSaysWhoCallsEachEventAndTheCellInWhichABehaviourCallsOneAgainstTheMachine
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00154-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, table
        status: approved
        title: A table diagram shall list what its ranges and its filter answer, a row each, with a cell under each column it writes
        text:
          diagram TB { kind: table; for <kind> <variable> [in <box>], ...; where <literals>;
          column <name>: <what> ... }. The rows are said as a rule says what it speaks about: every
          binding of the ranges that passes the filter. A column is a field of the row (r.title;
          name and id are what every entity has, holds says of a rule whether the model keeps it),
          what is related to the row (x where r satisfies x: every x that answers, by its name), or
          a yes or no (holds <literals>). The first column heads each row. A comparison reads a
          field of what a variable is bound to (r.status = "draft"), in a table and in a rule alike,
          and an artefact is in the box it is written in. A table written this way is the model's
          answer, kept when the design is checked; one that names a machine is REQ-00147-2. A cell
          too long for one line goes on several.
        rationale:
          Owner, 2026-11-17 (DS-268): one idea of table. The rows are a filter written as a rule and
          each column a question, so whatever a rule may range over a table may list: rules,
          requirements, use cases, constraints, classes. Nothing new to learn but the word column.
          Named tables and the cross (rows of one range, columns of another) come after it.
        verification VER-00170-2 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a table written with its rows and columns answers a cell for each, heads each row by its first column, says a yes or no, keeps only the rows its filter reads, and measures and wraps a cell by its characters
          status: draft
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_table_view.cpp::TestTableView.ATableWrittenWithRowsAndColumnsAnswersEachCell
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_table_view.cpp::TestTableSvg.ACellIsMeasuredByItsCharactersNotItsBytes
        }
        requirement REQ-00250-1 {
          tags: language, diagrams, table
          status: approved
          title: A column of a table shall read what the entity of its row is as a rule reads it: name(x), id(x) and kind(x)
          text:
            column <name>: name(<variable>) | id(<variable>) | kind(<variable>). The cell is what
            the entity bound to the variable is, as a literal of a rule reads it (REQ-00196-1):
            its name, its full identity, or its kind as the language writes it.
          rationale: DS-437, found by first-time users (DS-432): kind(r) in a column was an E-SYNTAX whose message named column while refusing the line. A table reads as a rule reads.
          verification VER-00270-1 {
            title: Integration tests confirm a column reads name(x), id(x) and kind(x) of the entity of its row, and the table binds with no syntax error
            status: approved
            method: test
            tests:
              tests/integration/ds/render/test_table_view.cpp::TestTableView.AColumnReadsWhatTheEntityOfTheRowIsAsARuleDoes
          }
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00122-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams, artefacts
        status: approved
        title: A requirement diagram shall draw whatever answers to a requirement, a member through the class or interface that holds it
        text:
          Every source of satisfies and constrained-by is drawn under the artefact: a package, a module,
          an interface, a class, an enum, a function, a use case, a rule. A method, an attribute, an
          operator or an operation answers through the class or the interface that holds it, and a
          holder and its member that answer the same artefact draw one edge.
        rationale:
          Model audit 2026-11, finding G11 (DS-220): nine pieces answered to one requirement and the
          diagram drew one, the class. The high-level design was the most affected: the box that says
          which requirements of the system it realises was not drawn under them. Members are lifted so
          the whole system's diagram stays readable.
        verification VER-00138-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm whatever answers to a requirement is drawn and a member answers through its holder, once
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_requirements_view.cpp::TestRequirementsViewOfAnyAnswer.WhateverAnswersToARequirementIsDrawnAndAMemberThroughItsHolder
        }
      }
      requirement REQ-00050-1 {
        tags: language, diagrams
        status: approved
        title: A requirement diagram shall show the umbrella of an artefact: what refines, verifies and realises it
        verification VER-00038-1 {
          title: Integration tests confirm a requirement diagram shows the umbrella of an artefact: what refines, verifies and realises it
          status: approved
          method: test
          tests:
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_requirements_view.cpp::TestRequirementsView.TheUmbrellaOfARequirement
            tests/integration/ds/render/test_requirements_view.cpp::TestRequirementsViewOfAUseCase.AUseCaseThatRealisesARequirementIsDrawnAsItsCard
        }
      }
    }

  }

  verification VER-00042-2 {
    status: approved
    @constraint CST-00002-1
    title: Integration tests confirm a second declaration of one identity is reported and reopenable scopes are not
    method: test
    tests:
      tests/integration/ds/bind/test_binder.cpp::TestBinder.ModulesCanBeReopenedAcrossFilesButClassesCannot
      tests/integration/ds/model/test_model_of_a_design.cpp::TestModelOfADesign.ASecondDeclarationIsReportedWithWhereTheFirstIs
  }
  verification VER-00043-2 {
    status: approved
    @constraint CST-00003-2
    title: Unit tests confirm an extension adds relations without redefining, an extension of nothing is expected, and a lone class of annotations is its definition
    method: test
    tests:
      tests/integration/ds/bind/test_packages.cpp::TestPackages.AnExtensionAddsRelationsWithoutRedefining
      tests/integration/ds/bind/test_packages.cpp::TestPackages.AnExtensionOfNothingIsExpected
      tests/integration/ds/bind/test_packages.cpp::TestLoneExtension.AClassWithRequirementsAndNoMembersIsADefinitionWhenNothingElseDefinesIt
  }
  verification VER-00044-2 {
    status: approved
    @constraint CST-00004-1
    title: Integration tests confirm packages qualify with @ and modules with ::
    text: Revision 2 (DS-184) cites the integration test alone; the unit tests of a name are the class's own.
    method: test
    tests:
      tests/integration/ds/bind/test_binder.cpp::TestBinder.PackagesQualifyWithAtAndModulesWithColons
  }
  verification VER-00045-1 {
    status: approved
    @constraint CST-00005-1
    title: Unit tests confirm a modifier is a word only in front of a name
    method: test
    tests:
      tests/integration/ds/bind/test_packages.cpp::TestPackages.AModifierIsAWordOnlyInFrontOfAName
  }
  verification VER-00046-1 {
    status: approved
    @constraint CST-00006-1
    title: Unit tests confirm an unpinned link is an error and a drifted one resolves to the declared revision
    method: test
    tests:
      tests/integration/ds/check/test_artefacts.cpp::TestArtefacts.TheRevisionIsPartOfTheIdentityAndLinksNameIt
  }
}

diagram REQ_Language {
  kind: requirement
  include: REQ-00012-1
  show: requirements
}

diagram REQ_LanguageVerified {
  kind: requirement
  include: REQ-00012-1
  show: verifications
}
